window.EPISODES = [{"id": "ep01", "num": 1, "title": "The Brass Vessel", "subtitle": "A king, a ring, and seventy-two things that would not kneel", "blurb": "Where the story starts: Solomon seals seventy-two spirits in a jar of brass, and somebody breaks it open.", "mood": "crypt", "scene": "crypt", "audio": "audio/ep01.m4a", "duration": 513.2, "words": 1329, "segments": [{"i": 0, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Somewhere under Babylon there is a lake. Nobody has found it. That has never stopped anyone from telling you exactly what is at the bottom.", "start": 2.5, "end": 11.85}, {"i": 1, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "At the bottom, the story goes, there was a jar. A vessel of brass, about as tall as a child, sealed at the neck with a lid of lead, and stamped with a seal that nothing inside could bear to look at. And inside it, folded up like smoke, were seventy-two spirits and every soldier who served them.", "start": 12.6, "end": 32.45}, {"i": 2, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "These be the seventy-two mighty Kings and Princes which King Solomon commanded into a Vessel of Brass, together with their Legions.", "start": 33.2, "end": 41.35, "src": "Goetia (1904), closing note"}, {"i": 3, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "That voice is the book. You will hear it again. Whenever it speaks, those are the real words, copied by hand in the sixteen hundreds, printed in nineteen oh four. I will never put words in its mouth. I am the other voice. Call me the Keeper. I hold the lamp, and I tell you where the stairs are.", "start": 42.4, "end": 63.75}, {"i": 4, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Good evening. This is The Brass Vessel. Twelve nights with one of the strangest books ever written in English. It has many names. The Lemegeton. The Clavicula Salomonis Regis. Most people call it the Lesser Key of Solomon. It is a catalogue of demons. Their names, their ranks, their faces, and what each one is good for.", "start": 64.5, "end": 89.55, "term": "lemegeton"}, {"i": 5, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Before we go down, three honest things. One. I am not going to teach you to summon anything, and I am not going to tell you this is real. Two. I am not going to tell you it is nonsense, either, because for four hundred years serious, frightened, clever people did not think so, and their fear is a kind of evidence about them. Three. You do not need to know anything. Not Latin, not scripture, not what a grimoire is. A grimoire is a book of magic. There. You are caught up.", "start": 90.3, "end": 122.15, "term": "grimoire"}, {"i": 6, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep01-t1", "v": "keeper", "t": "So tell me, before I open the cover. Why did you come down here tonight?", "start": 122.9, "end": 128.4, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "I want the history", "trait": "scholar", "reply": "Good. Then you will like the manuscripts, the forgeries, and the doctor who published a list of demons to prove that witches were innocent. History is the best part. History is where the bodies are. Keep your pencil sharp.", "audio": "audio/ep01-t1-a.m4a", "dur": 17.5}, {"key": "b", "label": "I want to feel something", "trait": "seeker", "reply": "Honest. Most people say history and mean this. Then listen with the lights low. The book was built to be felt. Every name in it was written to be spoken aloud, in the dark, by someone whose hands were shaking. We will go slowly.", "audio": "audio/ep01-t1-b.m4a", "dur": 19.3}, {"key": "c", "label": "I think it's nonsense", "trait": "skeptic", "reply": "Excellent. Stay that way. The first great printer of this list was a sceptic, and he printed it anyway, because he could not look away. Doubt is not a wall against this book. Doubt is how most people get in.", "audio": "audio/ep01-t1-c.m4a", "dur": 17.2}]}, {"i": 7, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Then we begin where the book begins. With a king.", "start": 129.4, "end": 133.25}, {"i": 8, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Solomon. Son of David, king in Jerusalem, some three thousand years ago. In scripture he is the wise one. He asks for wisdom instead of riches and receives both. He builds the Temple. That is the Solomon of Sunday school. But there is a second Solomon, who lives in the margins. And the second Solomon is a magician.", "start": 134.9, "end": 155.75, "mood": "court", "scene": "court"}, {"i": 9, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "The idea is old. By the first century, a historian named Josephus is already writing that God taught Solomon the art of casting out demons, and that he left behind incantations that still worked. A few centuries later, someone writes a wild little book called the Testament of Solomon. In it, a demon is stealing the wages and the life of a boy who works on the Temple. Solomon prays. And an angel hands him a ring.", "start": 156.5, "end": 181.9, "term": "testament"}, {"i": 10, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "Take, O Solomon, king, son of David, the gift which the Lord God has sent thee. With it thou shalt lock up all the demons of the earth, male and female; and with their help thou shalt build up Jerusalem.", "start": 182.65, "end": 196.05, "src": "Testament of Solomon"}, {"i": 11, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Read that last line again in your head. With their help. Solomon does not destroy the demons. He hires them. He stamps each one with the ring, asks its name, asks what it does, asks which angel it fears, and then he puts it to work cutting stone. The Temple of God, in this story, is built by a chain gang of devils. And notice the method. Name. Office. Weakness. That is an interview. That is a filing system. Remember it. The whole Lesser Key is that interview, written down seventy-two times.", "start": 197.1, "end": 233.25, "term": "ring"}, {"i": 12, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "So where does the brass vessel come in? Here is how the Lesser Key tells it, in a few plain lines near the end of its first book. Solomon gathers the seventy-two greatest spirits. Kings, dukes, princes. And he shuts them in the jar. And the book is careful to tell you why.", "start": 234.9, "end": 253.05, "mood": "crypt", "scene": "crypt"}, {"i": 13, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "And it is to be noted that Solomon did this because of their pride, for he never declared other reason why he thus bound them.", "start": 253.8, "end": 261.4, "src": "Goetia (1904), closing note"}, {"i": 14, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Because of their pride. Not murder. Not plague. Pride. I have always found that a little thrilling. Seventy-two beings who were asked to bow, and would not. Then the king seals the jar, and by divine power drives it down into a deep lake, or a hole, in Babylon. And that should be the end.", "start": 262.45, "end": 283.3, "term": "vessel"}, {"i": 15, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "It is not the end, because of treasure hunters. The people of Babylon see a king go to enormous trouble to sink a sealed brass jar, and they reach the only sensible conclusion. Gold. So they wade into the lake, and they haul it up, and they break the seal.", "start": 284.95, "end": 304.05, "mood": "sea", "scene": "sea"}, {"i": 16, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "But when they had broken it open, out flew the Chief Spirits immediately, with their Legions following them; and they were all restored to their former places.", "start": 304.8, "end": 314.05, "src": "Goetia (1904), closing note"}, {"i": 17, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "All of them, home again. All except one, called Belial, who climbed into a statue and began answering prayers, which is its own kind of joke. We will meet him on the fifth night. But understand what that ending does. It turns a myth into a manual. If the seventy-two are loose, then they are out there now. And if Solomon wrote down their names and their seals, then anyone holding the book is holding the lid of the jar.", "start": 316.0, "end": 345.85, "mood": "void", "scene": "void"}, {"i": 18, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep01-t2", "v": "keeper", "t": "Put yourself on that shore. The jar is in front of you, green with age, and something inside is very quiet. What do you do?", "start": 346.6, "end": 355.6, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "Break the seal", "trait": "seeker", "reply": "Of course you do. So did they. Every person who has ever opened this book has broken that seal, a little. I only ask that you notice the moment. That small cold drop in the stomach. That is the oldest feeling this book sells.", "audio": "audio/ep01-t2-a.m4a", "dur": 15.1}, {"key": "b", "label": "Read the seal first", "trait": "scholar", "reply": "Careful. I like that. That is the magician's answer, as it happens. The whole art, as the book describes it, is reading before touching. Names first. Circles first. The ones who rushed are not the ones who wrote the manuscripts.", "audio": "audio/ep01-t2-b.m4a", "dur": 18.6}, {"key": "c", "label": "Walk away", "trait": "skeptic", "reply": "Wise. And yet here you are, still listening. Walking away from the jar and walking away from the story about the jar are different skills. Very few people have the second one. I certainly do not.", "audio": "audio/ep01-t2-c.m4a", "dur": 15.6}]}, {"i": 19, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "So let us do the thing properly, and read one name. The first. The book opens its list without any ceremony at all. No warning, no preface. Just this.", "start": 357.5, "end": 368.95, "mood": "crypt", "scene": "crypt"}, {"i": 20, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "The First Principal Spirit is a King ruling in the East, called Bael. He maketh thee to go Invisible. He ruleth over sixty-six Legions of Infernal Spirits. He appeareth in divers shapes, sometimes like a Cat, sometimes like a Toad, and sometimes like a Man, and sometimes all these forms at once. He speaketh hoarsely.", "start": 369.7, "end": 391.7, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 1"}, {"i": 21, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Bael. A king in the east, with sixty-six legions. He comes as a cat, or a toad, or a man, or all three at once, and he speaks hoarsely. I love that the book bothers to tell you about his voice. And his gift is invisibility. Look at your grimoire now. His seal has just appeared in it. Every spirit I introduce will be kept there, so you can go back and look them in the eye.", "start": 392.75, "end": 418.9, "spirit": 1}, {"i": 22, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And notice the shape of that entry, because you are going to hear it seventy-one more times. A number. A rank. A name. A shape. A gift. A count of soldiers. It reads like a personnel file. That is the secret charm of the Lesser Key. It is not a scream. It is paperwork. Somebody sat down and made Hell into an office directory, and it is so calm about it that you start to believe the building exists.", "start": 419.65, "end": 446.9}, {"i": 23, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "One more thing about Bael, and then I will let you go. His name is older than this book. Much older. Thousands of years before anyone drew his seal, there was a storm god on the coast of Canaan, a rider of clouds, and his title was Baal. Lord. How a god of rain ends up as a hoarse king with a toad's head is one of the best stories we have, and I am saving it for the ninth night.", "start": 448.55, "end": 473.65, "mood": "storm", "scene": "storm"}, {"i": 24, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Tomorrow, the book itself. Because the Lesser Key is not one book. It is five, stitched together, and only the first is about demons. We will open the oldest copies, the ones in the British Library with the cramped brown ink, and ask the awkward question. Who actually wrote this?", "start": 475.3, "end": 494.1, "mood": "crypt", "scene": "crypt"}, {"i": 25, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Until then. The jar is open. The first name has been said. Seventy-one to go. Mind the stairs on your way up. And if you hear something hoarse behind you, do not worry. He is only being polite.", "start": 494.85, "end": 509.4}], "glossary": [{"id": "lemegeton", "term": "Lemegeton", "def": "The traditional name for the Lesser Key of Solomon, a seventeenth-century English collection of five books of ritual magic. Nobody is sure what the word means."}, {"id": "grimoire", "term": "Grimoire", "def": "A handbook of magic. The word is related to grammar: to people who could not read, any book of rules looked like sorcery."}, {"id": "testament", "term": "Testament of Solomon", "def": "A Greek text from the first centuries of the common era in which Solomon uses a magic ring to interrogate demons and force them to build the Temple. An ancestor of the Lesser Key."}, {"id": "ring", "term": "The Ring of Solomon", "def": "In legend, a signet ring given to Solomon by an angel, engraved with a seal that compels spirits to obey. Later imagined as a pentagram or hexagram."}, {"id": "vessel", "term": "The Brass Vessel", "def": "The sealed jar in which Solomon is said to have imprisoned the seventy-two spirits before sinking it in a lake in Babylon."}]}, {"id": "ep02", "num": 2, "title": "A Book With Five Doors", "subtitle": "Who wrote the Lesser Key, and what else is hiding in it", "blurb": "The Lesser Key turns out to be five books stitched into one, none of them by Solomon, and its first pages are gentler and odder than you were warned.", "mood": "library", "scene": "library", "audio": "audio/ep02.m4a", "duration": 705.8, "words": 1783, "segments": [{"i": 0, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Last night we left a brass jar lying open on a shore in Babylon. Tonight is quieter. Tonight there is a desk, a reading lamp, and a pencil, because they do not let you bring ink into this room.", "start": 2.5, "end": 16.6}, {"i": 1, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "On the desk is a brown manuscript. The British Library calls it Sloane thirty-eight twenty-five, which is a shelf number, not a title. The handwriting is cramped, seventeenth-century, the ink gone the colour of old tea. It is one of the earliest and most careful copies of the Lesser Key of Solomon that survives. And it raises the awkward question I promised you. If Solomon lived three thousand years ago, and this was copied out in England, in English, who actually wrote it?", "start": 17.35, "end": 48.1}, {"i": 2, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Before I answer, turn it over in your hands. In your mind, please. The first surprise is that this is not one book. It is five, stitched together under one name. I think of them as five doors along one corridor. Let me open each of them a crack.", "start": 48.85, "end": 67.3}, {"i": 3, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "The first door is the one you came for. The Ars Goetia. Ars is only Latin for art. Goetia is Greek, an old word for sorcery, the low and disreputable kind, and it is tied to a word for howling or wailing, the noise of someone crying over a grave. Behind this door are the seventy-two. The book itself describes the room quite cheerfully.", "start": 68.05, "end": 91.65, "term": "goetia"}, {"i": 4, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "The First Book, or Part, which is a Book concerning Spirits of Evil, and which is termed The Goetia of Solomon, sheweth forth his manner of binding these Spirits for use in things divers. And hereby did he acquire great renown.", "start": 92.4, "end": 107.2, "src": "Goetia (1904), preliminary definition"}, {"i": 5, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "The second door opens onto weather. The Ars Theurgia-Goetia. Here are thirty-one chief spirits of the air, emperors and dukes and wandering princes, each one assigned to a point of the compass, like the puffing faces of the winds on an old map. And the book is frank that they are a mixed lot.", "start": 109.15, "end": 128.4, "mood": "storm", "scene": "storm"}, {"i": 6, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "The Second Book is one which treateth of Spirits mingled of Good and Evil Natures, the which is entitled The Theurgia-Goetia, or the Magical Wisdom of the Spirits Aerial, whereof some do abide, but certain do wander and abide not.", "start": 129.15, "end": 146.3, "src": "Goetia (1904), preliminary definition"}, {"i": 7, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Some abide, and some wander. Now a documented fact, not a legend. Those names were lifted from a book called the Steganographia, written around fifteen hundred by a German abbot, Johannes Trithemius. It looked like a manual for sending messages by spirit. Much of it turned out to be cryptography, secret writing dressed up as angels. So the second door may open onto a codebook in a costume.", "start": 147.35, "end": 174.95, "term": "steganographia"}, {"i": 8, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "The third door is brighter. The Ars Paulina, the Pauline art, named for the apostle Paul. No demons here. Angels. An angel for each hour of the day and each hour of the night. And an angel for every single degree of the zodiac, three hundred and sixty of them, so that whatever moment you were born, somebody was on duty.", "start": 176.6, "end": 198.85, "mood": "dawn", "scene": "dawn"}, {"i": 9, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "The fourth door is the Ars Almadel, and it is the prettiest room in the house. An almadel is a square tablet of wax, cut with holy names, with a candle standing at each corner. Above it, the book says, appear the angels of four altitudes, four heights of heaven. One is described as coming like a young child dressed in rose-coloured satin, crowned with flowers. I am not making that up. I could not.", "start": 199.6, "end": 226.6, "term": "almadel"}, {"i": 10, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And the fifth door is the oldest by far. The Ars Notoria, the notary art. These are medieval prayers, already passing from hand to hand in the twelve hundreds, and what they promise is every student's daydream. Say them rightly, and you receive instant learning. Grammar, logic, a perfect memory, in weeks instead of years. Theologians condemned it, which did wonders for its circulation. And here is a tell. Some copies of the Lesser Key leave this fifth book out entirely.", "start": 227.35, "end": 259.6, "term": "ars-notoria"}, {"i": 11, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "So. Demons. Spirits of the winds. Angels of the hours. A wax tablet and four candles. Prayers for a perfect memory. Four of the five doors have nothing infernal behind them at all, and those are the four that nobody remembers.", "start": 260.35, "end": 277.95}, {"i": 12, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep02-t1", "v": "keeper", "t": "Five doors in one corridor, and tonight you may open only one of them. Which is it?", "start": 278.7, "end": 285.05, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "The prayers for memory", "trait": "scholar", "reply": "The fifth door. Of course. You would trade every demon in the catalogue for one night of remembering everything you ever read. So would most of the monks who copied it. That is the quiet scandal of the Ars Notoria. Its keenest readers were not sorcerers. They were students, with examinations coming.", "audio": "audio/ep02-t1-a.m4a", "dur": 20.1}, {"key": "b", "label": "The wax and the candles", "trait": "poet", "reply": "The Almadel. You chose the room for its light, and I cannot fault you. Four small flames, warm wax, and a child in rose satin somewhere above the table. It is the gentlest picture in the whole collection. Hardly anyone who buys the book ever reads that far.", "audio": "audio/ep02-t1-b.m4a", "dur": 18.5}, {"key": "c", "label": "The demons, still", "trait": "seeker", "reply": "Honest. So did the printers, and so has nearly every reader since. The first door has the worst reputation and by far the best stories, and it is no accident that it stands at the front. Good. That is the door we will be walking through for ten more nights.", "audio": "audio/ep02-t1-c.m4a", "dur": 18.6}]}, {"i": 13, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Whichever you chose, close it gently. There is still a harder question lying on the desk. Who wrote this? Not Solomon. I will say that plainly, once. There is no ancient scroll, nothing within two and a half thousand years of him. What there is, is a compilation, put together in England around the middle of the sixteen hundreds, by someone we cannot name, out of older pieces. One part of the text even lets slip the year sixteen forty-one, which is careless, for a king who died before Rome was founded.", "start": 286.95, "end": 321.05, "mood": "library", "scene": "library"}, {"i": 14, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And you can see the seams. The list of seventy-two comes largely from a catalogue printed in Latin in fifteen seventy-seven by a physician named Johann Weyer, under the wonderful title Pseudomonarchia Daemonum, the false kingdom of demons. It reached our compiler by way of Reginald Scot, who put it into English in fifteen eighty-four. Both were, in their way, debunkers. That story belongs to the tenth night, and it is a good one.", "start": 321.8, "end": 349.4}, {"i": 15, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "The copies themselves are all late. The one on this desk, Sloane thirty-eight twenty-five. Its neighbour, Sloane twenty-seven thirty-one, which carries the date sixteen eighty-seven and leaves out the fifth book. And Harley sixty-four eighty-three, later still, from the early seventeen hundreds. Then, in nineteen oh four, two English occultists, Samuel Mathers and Aleister Crowley, put it into print. But only the first book. Only the demons.", "start": 350.15, "end": 381.95}, {"i": 16, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "So why sign it Solomon? For the same reason a cookbook wants a famous chef on the cover. Authority. A spell by nobody is a scribble. A spell by the wisest king who ever lived is a tradition. Scholars call writing that borrows a great name pseudepigrapha. It is the oldest trick in publishing, and I doubt our compiler thought of it as fraud. More as good manners.", "start": 382.7, "end": 407.45, "term": "pseudepigrapha"}, {"i": 17, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "One last label to peel off. Why the Lesser Key? Lesser than what? There is another famous grimoire called simply the Key of Solomon, and to keep the two apart, people call that one the Greater Key. It is a different book. Older, born on the continent, and mostly concerned with planetary talismans, the right figure made at the right hour. It has no catalogue of seventy-two. People confuse the two constantly, including people who sell them. Now you will not.", "start": 408.2, "end": 439.35, "term": "greater-key"}, {"i": 18, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep02-t2", "v": "keeper", "t": "So there is the book with its mask off. Not three thousand years old. Under four hundred, and stitched together from borrowed parts. Tell me honestly. Does that make it less interesting to you, or more?", "start": 440.1, "end": 454.1, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "Less. The spell is broken", "trait": "skeptic", "reply": "Fair. A forged signature is a forged signature. But notice that the copyists knew roughly what you know, and kept copying. Whatever held them at the desk, it was not the name on the cover. Stay a little longer and see whether it gets hold of you too. I will not tell anyone.", "audio": "audio/ep02-t2-a.m4a", "dur": 21.0}, {"key": "b", "label": "More. Now it is a mystery", "trait": "scholar", "reply": "I hoped you would say so. A book handed down by a king is only an heirloom. A book assembled in secret, by an unknown English hand, from a doctor's appendix and an abbot's cipher, is a detective story. And we have ten nights left in which to dust it for fingerprints.", "audio": "audio/ep02-t2-b.m4a", "dur": 22.0}, {"key": "c", "label": "It never needed to be true", "trait": "poet", "reply": "Then you read the way the book wants to be read. Nobody asks whether a ghost story was written by the ghost. The name Solomon is not a claim. It is a candle set in the window, so that you know what kind of house you are walking into.", "audio": "audio/ep02-t2-c.m4a", "dur": 16.0}]}, {"i": 19, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Either way, the pages are the same pages. And last night we stopped after a single name. So let us turn the leaf, and see who the book thought should come directly after Bael. I think they will surprise you.", "start": 456.0, "end": 470.05, "mood": "court", "scene": "court"}, {"i": 20, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He is under the Power of the East, and cometh up in the form of an old fair Man, riding upon a Crocodile, carrying a Goshawk upon his fist, and yet mild in appearance. He maketh them to run that stand still, and bringeth back runaways.", "start": 470.8, "end": 487.7, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 2"}, {"i": 21, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Agares. The second spirit, a duke, with thirty-one legions. An old man, fair to look at, riding a crocodile, with a goshawk on his fist. And yet mild in appearance, the book adds, as if it knew how the crocodile would come across. He makes those who stand still run, and he brings runaways back. He teaches every language at once. He pulls down the dignified, churchmen and lords alike. And he causes earthquakes. A kindly grandfather who can shake the ground. His seal is in your grimoire now.", "start": 488.75, "end": 523.35, "spirit": 2}, {"i": 22, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Turn the page. The third spirit is a prince named Vassago, with twenty-six legions, and he has no face. Truly. The book gives him no shape at all. It says only that he is of the same nature as Agares, and then it says this.", "start": 524.1, "end": 540.0, "spirit": 3}, {"i": 23, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "This Spirit is of a Good Nature, and his office is to declare things Past and to Come, and to discover all things Hid or Lost.", "start": 540.75, "end": 550.4, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 3"}, {"i": 24, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Of a good nature. In a catalogue of demons. He tells what has been and what will be, and he finds whatever is hidden or lost. And one small honest footnote. Vassago is not in Weyer's printed list at all. He joins the company later, from somewhere nobody has traced. The good-natured one is the one with no known parents.", "start": 551.45, "end": 571.1}, {"i": 25, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And the fourth. Change the air for this one. Imagine a shoreline at night. Cold water, no moon. Something small is standing in the shallows, and it is not what you expected to find in a book of devils.", "start": 572.75, "end": 587.85, "mood": "sea", "scene": "sea"}, {"i": 26, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He appeareth in the form of a little Horse or Ass, and then into Human shape doth he change himself at the request of the Master. He speaketh with a hoarse voice.", "start": 588.6, "end": 598.5, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 4"}, {"i": 27, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Samigina. A marquis, with thirty legions. A little horse, or a donkey, who becomes a man if he is asked, and who is hoarse, like Bael. Perhaps it is the damp down there. He teaches all the liberal sciences, the old school subjects, grammar and geometry and the rest. And he gives account of dead souls that died in sin. In the older version that Weyer printed, it is stranger and sadder. There he answers for the souls of those who drowned in the sea. A small rough-voiced horse at the edge of the water, telling you how they are.", "start": 599.55, "end": 637.25, "spirit": 4}, {"i": 28, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Notice what we have just read. An invisible king. A mild old man. A kindly finder of lost things. A talking horse who knows his grammar. Four pages into the most notorious demon book in the English language, and nobody has been torn apart. It gets darker. But the catalogue is gentler and odder than its reputation, and I wanted you to feel that early.", "start": 638.0, "end": 659.85}, {"i": 29, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Tomorrow night, the furniture. Because the magician in these pages never simply calls a spirit and hopes. He stands inside one shape, and he makes the spirit stand inside another. The Circle and the Triangle. We will ask what they were for, and what it tells you about a man that he draws a wall around himself before he says hello.", "start": 661.5, "end": 683.8, "mood": "crypt", "scene": "crypt"}, {"i": 30, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Until then. Three new seals in your grimoire, and five doors in the corridor behind you, four of them still shut. Leave the manuscript on the desk. Pencils only. And if a small horse clears its throat on your way out, be kind. He has had a long night.", "start": 684.55, "end": 702.0}], "glossary": [{"id": "goetia", "term": "Ars Goetia", "def": "The first of the five books of the Lesser Key, and the only one about demons: a catalogue of seventy-two spirits. Goetia is a Greek word for sorcery, linked to a word for howling or wailing."}, {"id": "steganographia", "term": "Steganographia", "def": "A book written around 1500 by the German abbot Johannes Trithemius. It reads like spirit magic but much of it conceals a system of secret writing. The second book of the Lesser Key borrows its spirit names."}, {"id": "almadel", "term": "Almadel", "def": "A square wax tablet inscribed with holy names, with a candle at each corner, described in the fourth book of the Lesser Key as the place where angels of the four altitudes appear."}, {"id": "ars-notoria", "term": "Ars Notoria", "def": "The notary art: medieval prayers, in circulation by the thirteenth century, that promise rapid learning and a perfect memory. It became the fifth book of the Lesser Key, though some manuscripts omit it."}, {"id": "pseudepigrapha", "term": "Pseudepigrapha", "def": "Writings credited to a famous figure who did not write them. Attaching Solomon's name to a seventeenth-century English compilation is a classic case."}, {"id": "greater-key", "term": "The Greater Key", "def": "The Key of Solomon, an older and entirely separate grimoire concerned mostly with planetary talismans. The Lesser Key is so called to tell the two apart."}]}, {"id": "ep03", "num": 3, "title": "The Circle and the Triangle", "subtitle": "Chalk, parchment, and the manners of the terrified", "blurb": "The stage set of the Goetia: a nine-foot circle, a triangle outside it, a ring against bad breath, and a procedure that reads like a nervous lawyer's day in court.", "mood": "crypt", "scene": "crypt", "audio": "audio/ep03.m4a", "duration": 702.5, "words": 1782, "segments": [{"i": 0, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "A stone floor. A piece of chalk. A circle, nine feet across, drawn with great care by someone whose hand is not quite steady. And in the middle of it, a person in a white robe, who has just understood the one rule of the evening. Whatever happens next, you do not step over the line.", "start": 2.5, "end": 22.55}, {"i": 1, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Good evening. Last night we counted the five doors of this book. Tonight we walk through the first and look at the furniture. Because before the Goetia lets anyone call a single spirit, it spends pages on equipment. And let me say it plainly. I am describing a stage set, as a historian would. This is not a shopping list.", "start": 23.3, "end": 45.6}, {"i": 2, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "This is the Form of the Magical Circle of King Solomon, the which he made that he might preserve himself therein from the malice of these Evil Spirits.", "start": 46.35, "end": 55.35, "src": "Goetia (1904), The Magical Circle"}, {"i": 3, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Preserve himself. The first piece of equipment in the most famous demon book in English is not a weapon. It is a fence. Nine feet across, with the names of God written all the way round it. In the famous drawing, those Hebrew names ride along a coiled serpent. The editor admits, in a footnote, that only one private manuscript shows the serpent. The snake is decoration. The names are the wall.", "start": 56.4, "end": 79.2, "term": "magic-circle"}, {"i": 4, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And then, outside the wall, a second shape. A triangle. Two feet from the circle, three feet across, pointing toward whichever quarter of the world the spirit belongs to. This is where the visitor is meant to stand. Magicians call it the triangle of art. Holy names run along its sides, and across the middle, in three pieces, is the name Michael. The archangel who, in scripture, fights the dragon.", "start": 79.95, "end": 105.8, "term": "triangle-of-art"}, {"i": 5, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Look at the geometry and you have the whole idea. You in here. It out there. Between you, a wall made of words. The book never suggests the magician is stronger than a demon. It suggests he has better references.", "start": 106.55, "end": 121.05}, {"i": 6, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Then there is the wardrobe. A long white robe of linen. On the breast, a five-pointed star, the pentagram of Solomon, in gold or silver, with the spirit's own seal on the back. And at the skirt of the robe, on calfskin parchment, a six-pointed star, the hexagram of Solomon, under a linen cloth. It is shown to the spirits, says the text, to compel them into human shape.", "start": 122.7, "end": 150.2, "mood": "library", "scene": "library", "term": "hexagram"}, {"i": 7, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "My favourite object is the smallest. The book calls it the magic ring, then corrects itself. Or rather disc, it says. Gold or silver. Exorcist, by the way, is simply this book's word for the magician. And the job of the ring is wonderfully practical.", "start": 150.95, "end": 167.45}, {"i": 8, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "It is to be held before the face of the exorcist to preserve him from the stinking sulphurous fumes and flaming breath of the Evil Spirits.", "start": 168.2, "end": 176.85, "src": "Goetia (1904), The Magic Ring or Disc of Solomon"}, {"i": 9, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "A gas mask, in other words. Add a sword, a dish of burning charcoal, and a secret seal, which the text wants perfumed with alum, raisins dried in the sun, dates and cedar. Somebody thought hard about the raisins. That is the texture of this book. Terror, itemised.", "start": 177.9, "end": 196.8}, {"i": 10, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep03-t1", "v": "keeper", "t": "So. You are standing in the circle, and I will let you hold one thing. Only one. Which do you want in your hand?", "start": 197.55, "end": 205.15, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "The ring, for the breath", "trait": "skeptic", "reply": "Practical. You chose the one object with a job description. No names of God, no stars, just something between your nose and the smell. I suspect the copyists felt the same. It is the only item on the list that the book bothers to bring up again, twice, by name.", "audio": "audio/ep03-t1-a.m4a", "dur": 19.3}, {"key": "b", "label": "The sword", "trait": "seeker", "reply": "Of course. Something with an edge. I will tell you a secret, though. In this book the sword is barely a weapon at all. Wait a few minutes and you will see what actually gets hung on the point of it. It is not what you are hoping for.", "audio": "audio/ep03-t1-b.m4a", "dur": 15.6}, {"key": "c", "label": "The star under its cloth", "trait": "poet", "reply": "The hexagram. So what you want is not safety. You want the moment the linen comes away and the thing in the triangle has to put on a face. That is the most theatrical gesture in the whole book, and you went straight to it. I am keeping an eye on you.", "audio": "audio/ep03-t1-c.m4a", "dur": 16.9}]}, {"i": 11, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Hold on to it. Because now the room is ready, and the book turns from furniture to procedure. And the procedure sounds less like a spell than like a day in court.", "start": 207.05, "end": 219.05, "mood": "court", "scene": "court"}, {"i": 12, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "It opens with a conjuration. That is a formal summons, spoken aloud, calling a spirit by its name and by the names of God. The first one is long, grand, and very particular about its terms. The spirit is to come at once, from anywhere in the world. Peaceably. And looking presentable.", "start": 219.8, "end": 239.3, "term": "conjuration"}, {"i": 13, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "That thou dost forthwith appear unto me here before this Circle in a fair human shape, without any deformity or tortuosity.", "start": 240.05, "end": 249.55, "src": "Goetia (1904), The Conjuration"}, {"i": 14, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "No deformity. No tortuosity. And a few lines on, a voice clear and intelligible, without any ambiguity. Think what is being feared there. Not fire. Not claws. Mumbling. Answers you cannot use.", "start": 250.6, "end": 266.95}, {"i": 15, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And if nothing happens? The book has planned for that, with the patience of a clerk. There is a second conjuration, mostly a list of divine names and their service records. At this name the rivers turned to blood. This one brought the frogs. At this one the sun stood still. Then comes my favourite stage direction in the book.", "start": 267.7, "end": 289.25}, {"i": 16, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "If he come not yet at the rehearsal of these two first conjurations, but without doubt he will, say on as followeth; it being a constraint.", "start": 290.0, "end": 299.7, "src": "Goetia (1904), The Second Conjuration"}, {"i": 17, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "But without doubt he will. You can hear the author reassuring himself. The constraint drops the courtesy. Now there are threats. Loss of office. The bottomless pit. And if that fails, the book offers a very human explanation. The spirit has been sent elsewhere by his king, and cannot come. So you go over his head, and ask the king to send him along.", "start": 300.75, "end": 326.0}, {"i": 18, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Still nothing? Then, says the text, he must be lying in chains in hell, and for that there is a general curse, called the Spirits' Chain, at which the chains are supposed to break. And past that lies the last resort. It is the pettiest, most vivid thing in the book.", "start": 327.65, "end": 345.4, "mood": "fire", "scene": "fire", "term": "spirits-chain"}, {"i": 19, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "Write thou his seal on parchment and put thou it into a strong black box; with brimstone, assafoetida, and such like things that bear a stinking smell; and then bind the box up round with an iron wire.", "start": 346.15, "end": 360.45, "src": "Goetia (1904), The Spirits' Chain"}, {"i": 20, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "The box is hung on the point of the sword, over the charcoal. A spirit's name, held hostage above a fire, packed in bad smells. An editor's footnote adds, helpfully, that the box should be something that does not burn easily. And then, the moment the spirit arrives, the fire is put out, a sweet perfume is made, and the visitor is given, in the book's words, welcome and a kind entertainment.", "start": 361.5, "end": 389.25}, {"i": 21, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Threats, then hospitality. Then the questions. And then, every single time, the ending, which the book calls the license to depart. A formal dismissal. Go home, and harm neither man nor beast on the way. Even then, the magician may not leave the circle until the spirit is gone. Remember that phrase. Our last night together is named for it.", "start": 390.0, "end": 411.6, "term": "license-to-depart"}, {"i": 22, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "So. Why does a book of forbidden magic read like the minutes of a meeting? Here is what I think, and it is only what I think. This is fear management. The people who copied these pages in the sixteen hundreds mostly believed the things on the list could hurt them. So they reached for the two systems they trusted. The church, and the law.", "start": 413.25, "end": 433.8, "mood": "void", "scene": "void"}, {"i": 23, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Listen to the vocabulary. Bonds. Charges. Office. License. A spirit has a rank, a king above him, and a proper form of address. This is feudal society, projected onto the invisible. And the magician is no wizard throwing lightning. He is a nervous lawyer with a subpoena, reading out a higher authority's name and hoping it is recognised.", "start": 434.55, "end": 459.7}, {"i": 24, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And underneath the law, something far older. Draw a line. Stand behind it. Say, that is out there, and I am in here. Children do it with blankets. Towns did it with walls. A circle on the floor may be the oldest gesture we have. A line between us and the dark.", "start": 460.45, "end": 479.95}, {"i": 25, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep03-t2", "v": "keeper", "t": "So let me ask you. The summons has been read. Something is standing in the triangle, just past the chalk. What do you do?", "start": 480.7, "end": 488.6, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "Stay in. Eyes down.", "trait": "skeptic", "reply": "Sensible. And notice that you did not say you would step out and prove the triangle empty. Nobody ever says that. The circle works on doubters too. It does not need you to believe in the visitor. It only needs you to prefer, on balance, not to find out.", "audio": "audio/ep03-t2-a.m4a", "dur": 17.5}, {"key": "b", "label": "Look it in the face", "trait": "seeker", "reply": "I thought you might. The book is on your side, as it happens. All that insistence on a fair human shape exists so that someone like you can look. Just keep your toes behind the chalk while you do it. Curiosity is allowed in here. Leaning is not.", "audio": "audio/ep03-t2-b.m4a", "dur": 18.3}, {"key": "c", "label": "Start asking questions", "trait": "scholar", "reply": "Straight to business. That is exactly what the text expects. Once the welcome is said, it tells the exorcist to state his desires and requests. No small talk. You would have made a fine conjuror, or a fine magistrate. In this book they are nearly the same profession.", "audio": "audio/ep03-t2-c.m4a", "dur": 16.8}]}, {"i": 26, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Whatever you chose, you would want to know who was standing there. So here are two entries where the book itself shows why the furniture matters. The first, you would hear before you saw.", "start": 490.5, "end": 501.95, "mood": "storm", "scene": "storm"}, {"i": 27, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He is a mighty King and terrible. He rideth on a pale horse with trumpets and other kinds of musical instruments playing before him. He is very furious at his first appearance.", "start": 502.7, "end": 514.4, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 13"}, {"i": 28, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Beleth. The thirteenth spirit. A king, with eighty-five legions behind him, and he arrives in a rage. Now watch the equipment appear. The exorcist, says the book, must hold a hazel wand, stretch it toward the south and east, and command him into a triangle outside the circle. All the while he keeps a silver ring on the middle finger of his left hand, held against his face. His seal is in your grimoire now. And then, the line that gives the game away.", "start": 515.45, "end": 545.9, "spirit": 13}, {"i": 29, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "Yet he must receive him courteously because he is a Great King, and do homage unto him, as the Kings and Princes do that attend upon him.", "start": 546.65, "end": 555.2, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 13"}, {"i": 30, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Homage. To someone you have just threatened into a chalk triangle with a stick. That is the etiquette of this book in one sentence. You may compel a king, but you may not be rude to him. And what does he do, once the trumpets stop? He causes love. All the love that may be, both of men and of women. The most frightening entrance in the catalogue is a matchmaker's.", "start": 556.25, "end": 581.7}, {"i": 31, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "The second is Astaroth, the twenty-ninth spirit. A mighty, strong duke, ruling forty legions. He appears as a hurtful angel, riding an infernal beast like a dragon and carrying a viper in his right hand. And here the ring stops being jewellery.", "start": 582.45, "end": 600.35, "spirit": 29}, {"i": 32, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "Thou must in no wise let him approach too near unto thee, lest he do thee damage by his Noisome Breath. Wherefore the Magician must hold the Magical Ring near his face, and that will defend him.", "start": 601.1, "end": 615.0, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 29"}, {"i": 33, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "His breath. Of all the things to fear from an angel on a dragon. Yet the reward is remarkable. Astaroth gives true answers about things past, present and to come. He discovers all secrets, and makes men wonderfully knowing in the liberal sciences. And, if asked, he will tell you how the spirits fell, and the reason for his own fall. Imagine that interview, conducted from behind a little disc.", "start": 616.05, "end": 645.0}, {"i": 34, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "So that is the room. A circle to stand in. A triangle to point at. A ring for the smell. And a script that begins with a polite summons and ends with a box over a fire. None of it tells you whether anything ever came. It tells you how carefully people were afraid.", "start": 646.65, "end": 665.6, "mood": "crypt", "scene": "crypt"}, {"i": 35, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Tomorrow night, the guests themselves, in order of precedence. Because Beleth is a king and Astaroth is a duke, and in this book that matters. Marquises, presidents, earls, each with a count of legions, and above them, four kings of the four quarters. The Courts of Hell.", "start": 666.35, "end": 685.25}, {"i": 36, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Until then. Rub out the chalk, if you like. But do it from the inside, and do it last. And if you hear trumpets on the stairs, stand up straight. He is a great king, and he does notice.", "start": 686.0, "end": 698.7}], "glossary": [{"id": "magic-circle", "term": "The Magical Circle", "def": "The ring drawn on the floor, nine feet across in the Goetia and surrounded by divine names, inside which the magician stands for protection. The spirit is never meant to enter it."}, {"id": "triangle-of-art", "term": "Triangle of Art", "def": "A triangle set two feet outside the circle, bearing holy names and the name of the archangel Michael. It marks the place where the summoned spirit is supposed to appear and stay."}, {"id": "hexagram", "term": "Hexagram and Pentagram of Solomon", "def": "Two star figures worn by the magician: a six-pointed one on parchment at the skirt of the robe, shown to make spirits take human shape, and a five-pointed one in gold or silver on the breast."}, {"id": "conjuration", "term": "Conjuration", "def": "A formal spoken summons that calls a spirit by its own name and commands it by the names of God. The Goetia gives several, each sterner than the last."}, {"id": "spirits-chain", "term": "The Spirits' Chain", "def": "The Goetia's name for a general curse read against a spirit that refuses to appear. It is followed, as a last resort, by burning the spirit's seal in a box of foul-smelling things."}, {"id": "license-to-depart", "term": "License to Depart", "def": "The formal dismissal that ends every working in the book: the spirit is thanked and told to return peaceably to its place, harming no one."}]}, {"id": "ep04", "num": 4, "title": "The Courts of Hell", "subtitle": "Seven ranks, seven metals, and exactly one knight", "blurb": "Hell turns out to be a feudal kingdom with titles, office hours and a head count, and tonight you meet the middle of its table.", "mood": "court", "scene": "court", "audio": "audio/ep04.m4a", "duration": 715.0, "words": 1839, "segments": [{"i": 0, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Picture a banquet hall with no walls you can see. One long table, running off into the dark. At every place there is a small card with a name on it, and under the name, a title. Last night I showed you the circle and the triangle, the furniture on your side of the line. Tonight we look across it. Hell, it turns out, has a seating plan.", "start": 2.5, "end": 25.75}, {"i": 1, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "This is the thing nobody expects from the Lesser Key. You open a book of demons braced for chaos. What you get is a kingdom. Kings, and under them dukes, princes, marquises, earls. The titles are lifted straight from the courts of Europe. The men who built this list could imagine a great many horrors. They could not imagine a place without a nobility.", "start": 26.5, "end": 49.75}, {"i": 2, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "There are seven ranks. Kings. Dukes. Princes, whom the book sometimes calls prelates, a word for a senior churchman. Marquises. Presidents, which sounds modern and is not. It meant someone who presides, the head of a council or a college. Earls, also called counts. And at the very bottom, one knight. Not an order of knights. One. He closes the night.", "start": 50.5, "end": 75.6, "term": "president"}, {"i": 3, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Go by the entries, and the seventy-two share out like this. Nine kings. Twenty-three dukes, the biggest crowd in the room. Seven princes. Fifteen marquises. Fourteen presidents. Twelve earls. One knight. That adds up to eighty-one, and there are only seventy-two spirits. Nobody miscounted. Nine of them hold two titles at once.", "start": 76.35, "end": 98.85}, {"i": 4, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "It will be remarked that several among the above Spirits possess two titles of different ranks.", "start": 99.6, "end": 106.5, "src": "Goetia (1904), editor's note to the classified list"}, {"i": 5, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Think of a count who also chairs a company, and would like both on the card, please. Six of those nine are on tonight's guest list. Tonight is the middle of the table, the ones with two jobs and no crown.", "start": 107.55, "end": 120.75}, {"i": 6, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Now the detail I love most. Each rank has a metal. The seals, those signatures collecting in your grimoire, are to be made in metal, the book says, and which metal depends on the title. Gold for kings, the metal of the sun. Copper for dukes, for Venus. Tin for princes, for Jupiter. Silver for marquises, for the moon. Lead for the knight, for Saturn.", "start": 122.4, "end": 146.65, "mood": "fire", "scene": "fire", "term": "planetary-metals"}, {"i": 7, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Then it gets odd. Presidents get mercury. Quicksilver. A seal made in a metal that runs across the table. And earls get copper and silver, mixed in equal parts. Seven ranks, seven planets of the old sky, and yet Mars and his iron are nowhere. The editor noticed. He suggests the earls were probably meant to have iron all along. Even Hell's paperwork has errata.", "start": 147.4, "end": 174.5}, {"i": 8, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep04-t1", "v": "keeper", "t": "So, before we go further down the table. If a card with your name on it were waiting there, which title would you want written underneath?", "start": 175.25, "end": 183.85, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "King, sealed in gold", "trait": "seeker", "reply": "Ambitious. Then know the terms. There are only nine crowns, the gold is real, and the book fences its kings into strict office hours, as you are about to hear. The head of the table is the most watched seat in the room. I would take it too, mind. I just would not expect to enjoy it.", "audio": "audio/ep04-t1-a.m4a", "dur": 19.3}, {"key": "b", "label": "President, in quicksilver", "trait": "scholar", "reply": "A good choice, and an underrated one. The presidents are the working experts of this book. They teach, they answer, they know where things are kept. Nobody writes ballads about a president. But when a king needs something understood, I suspect he sends for one. You will meet four of them tonight.", "audio": "audio/ep04-t1-b.m4a", "dur": 20.0}, {"key": "c", "label": "I'd refuse the seat", "trait": "skeptic", "reply": "Sensible. You have noticed that every chair at this table has another chair above it. Even the kings, as you will hear, answer to somebody. The only person in the whole arrangement without a title is the one holding the book. Keep that in mind. It may be the best seat in the house.", "audio": "audio/ep04-t1-c.m4a", "dur": 16.7}]}, {"i": 9, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Whichever card you chose, it comes with soldiers. Every entry in the catalogue ends the same way, with a count of legions. A legion was the great unit of the Roman army, a few thousand men. The Goetia never says how many spirits make an infernal one. It just hands you the totals, like a clerk. Sixty legions here. Nineteen there. Two hundred for the grandest of the kings.", "start": 185.75, "end": 211.8, "mood": "library", "scene": "library", "term": "legion"}, {"i": 10, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "An older version of the list is less shy. Reginald Scot printed it in English in fifteen eighty-four, as a sceptic, to show how absurd he found it. At the bottom of the roll he sets the reader a sum.", "start": 212.55, "end": 225.6}, {"i": 11, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "Note that a legion is six thousand six hundred and sixty-six, and now by multiplication count how many legions do arise out of every particular.", "start": 226.35, "end": 235.9, "src": "Reginald Scot, The Discoverie of Witchcraft (1584), book 15, chapter 2"}, {"i": 12, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "So let us play, and I do mean play. Add up every legion the Goetia prints and you pass two thousand six hundred. Multiply by Scot's figure. You get something over seventeen million spirits, under seventy-two commanders. That is play, not theology. But it feels like a census. Somebody counted. And the seventy-two are not the top of it. Above them, the book sets four more.", "start": 236.95, "end": 266.55}, {"i": 13, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "These seventy-two Kings be under the Power of Amaymon, Corson, Zimimay or Ziminiar, and Goap, who are the Four Great Kings ruling in the Four Quarters, or Cardinal Points.", "start": 268.2, "end": 281.8, "src": "Goetia (1904), Observations", "mood": "void", "scene": "void"}, {"i": 14, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "East, west, north and south, it goes on, in that order. Amaymon, Corson, Ziminiar, Goap. And they are not to be called, the book says, except upon great occasions. You do not summon the board. You ask the board to send someone down. Other grimoires give these four quite different names, which tells you how old the idea is, and how unsettled.", "start": 282.85, "end": 306.3, "term": "four-kings"}, {"i": 15, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Then the pettiest rule in the text, and my favourite. Each rank keeps hours. The book's word is bound, meaning the hours in which a spirit of that rank can be held to its task. Kings, from nine in the morning until noon, and again from three until sunset. So a king takes a long lunch. Here are some of the others.", "start": 307.05, "end": 327.7}, {"i": 16, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "Dukes may be bound from Sunrise till Noonday in Clear Weather; Prelates may be bound any hour of the Day; Knights may from Dawning of Day till Sunrise, or from four o'clock till Sunset.", "start": 328.45, "end": 341.25, "src": "Goetia (1904), Observations"}, {"i": 17, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Dukes, only in clear weather. And earls, the book adds, at any hour of the day, so long as it is in the woods, or some place where people do not go and there is no noise. An earl wants quiet.", "start": 342.3, "end": 355.95}, {"i": 18, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Now look at who has no card on the table. Read all seventy-two names and you will not find Satan, or Beelzebub. Lucifer has no entry and no seal. His name surfaces only in passing, inside the files of two kings you will meet tomorrow, the way a clerk mentions the owner. The management is offstage. The Goetia lists only the ones who, in the story, can be reached. So let us walk the table, starting with the one who knows the four great kings personally.", "start": 356.7, "end": 385.5}, {"i": 19, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He appeareth when the Sun is in some of the Southern Signs, in a Human Shape, going before Four Great and Mighty Kings, as if he were a Guide to conduct them along on their way.", "start": 387.15, "end": 398.75, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 33", "mood": "court", "scene": "court"}, {"i": 20, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Gaap. A great president and a mighty prince, with sixty-six legions. He can make you knowing in philosophy and the liberal sciences, or he can make you ignorant, whichever is asked. He causes love or hatred. He tells past, present and future, and carries people very speedily from one kingdom to another. He also delivers familiars out of the keeping of other magicians. A familiar is an attendant spirit, a supernatural servant. Apparently they can be poached. And yes, his name sounds like Goap, the king in the south. The book never untangles the two. Below him sit the earls, each with a second title.", "start": 399.8, "end": 439.7, "spirit": 33, "term": "familiar"}, {"i": 21, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He appeareth at the first show in the form of an ugly Viper, then at the command of the Magician he putteth on a Human shape with Great Teeth, and two Horns, carrying a bright and sharp Sword in his hand.", "start": 440.45, "end": 453.55, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 17"}, {"i": 22, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Botis. A great president and an earl, ruling sixty legions. The man with the teeth and the sword is not obviously an improvement on the viper. And his office? He tells all things past and to come, and he reconciles friends and foes. The viper is a peacemaker. This book does that constantly. The shape says run. The job description says mediator.", "start": 454.6, "end": 478.45, "spirit": 17}, {"i": 23, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Beside him, Marax. An earl and a president, with thirty legions, who appears as a great bull with a man's face. He makes people very knowing in astronomy and all the liberal sciences, the old name for a university education. And he gives good, wise familiars, who know the virtues of herbs and precious stones.", "start": 479.2, "end": 500.0, "spirit": 21}, {"i": 24, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Then Ipos, an earl and a mighty prince, with thirty-six legions. He arrives as an angel with a lion's head, a goose's foot and a hare's tail. He knows all things past, present and to come, and he makes men witty and bold. The book's own closing list forgets that he is an earl and files him with the princes only.", "start": 500.75, "end": 521.2, "spirit": 22}, {"i": 25, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He teacheth all Arts and Sciences in an instant, and is an Author of Bloodshed and Manslaughter.", "start": 521.95, "end": 528.4, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 25"}, {"i": 26, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "That is Glasya-Labolas. A mighty president and earl, thirty-six legions, who shows himself as a dog with wings like a gryphon. Teaching and manslaughter, in one breath, with no change of tone. He also tells things past and to come, causes the love of friends and foes, and can make a man invisible. The winged dog is the one guest tonight I would not seat near the knives.", "start": 529.45, "end": 555.35, "spirit": 25}, {"i": 27, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And Ronove, a marquis and a great earl, with nineteen legions. All the book will say of his looks is that he appears in the form of a monster. After lion heads and goose feet, the scribe simply gives up. But the monster teaches rhetoric very well, and gives good servants, knowledge of tongues, and favour with friends or foes. The least describable thing in the room is the one who teaches you to speak.", "start": 556.1, "end": 581.75, "spirit": 27}, {"i": 28, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep04-t2", "v": "keeper", "t": "You have seen what this court trades in. Soldiers, answers, and a way with words. If one of them offered you a single gift, which would you take?", "start": 582.5, "end": 592.95, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "Legions at my command", "trait": "seeker", "reply": "Then you want a king's portion, and you are in honest company. But look again at the files. Almost nobody in this book is asked for an army. The legions are listed like acreage, a measure of standing. What people asked for was smaller and closer to home. Still. A few thousand at your back on a dark road. I see the appeal.", "audio": "audio/ep04-t2-a.m4a", "dur": 24.5}, {"key": "b", "label": "To know things instantly", "trait": "scholar", "reply": "Of course. That is the request this book expects. Count the offices from tonight alone. Astronomy, philosophy, the liberal sciences, things past and to come. Whoever copied these pages wanted, more than gold, to stop being ignorant. I find that the most human thing in the whole catalogue. And the most dangerous, naturally.", "audio": "audio/ep04-t2-b.m4a", "dur": 23.1}, {"key": "c", "label": "Wit and a ready tongue", "trait": "poet", "reply": "Ah. The gift of Ipos, and of Ronove. Witty and bold. It sounds like the modest wish, and I think it is the shrewdest. Armies desert, and facts go stale. But the person who speaks well walks into any court, above or below, and is offered a chair. You may have it already.", "audio": "audio/ep04-t2-c.m4a", "dur": 21.8}]}, {"i": 29, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Whatever you took, there is one seat left, down at the cold end by the door. The book gives knights only the thin edges of the day. From first light until sunrise, or from four o'clock until the sun goes down. So picture that grey hour, and someone riding out of it.", "start": 594.85, "end": 613.0, "mood": "dawn", "scene": "dawn"}, {"i": 30, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He is a Knight, and appeareth in the Form of a Cruel Old Man with a long Beard and a hoary Head, riding upon a pale-coloured Horse, with a Sharp Weapon in his hand.", "start": 613.75, "end": 624.0, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 50"}, {"i": 31, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Furcas. The only knight among the seventy-two, with twenty legions and a seal in lead. Every instinct says executioner. And his office is to teach. Philosophy, astrology, rhetoric, logic, the reading of palms, and divination by fire, in all their parts, the book says, and perfectly. He is a professor. A frightening, underpromoted professor on a horse the colour of ash. His seal is in your grimoire now, beside the six who outrank him.", "start": 625.05, "end": 654.45, "spirit": 50}, {"i": 32, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "So why does it matter that Hell has a seating plan? Because of what a hierarchy does to fear. The unknown is hardest to bear when it has no shape. Give it titles, and metals, and office hours, and it becomes a place with procedures. If there are ranks, there are rules. If there are rules, they can be learned, by you, in a chair, with a book. Whether or not anything ever answered, that was the promise of these pages. The dark could be read.", "start": 656.1, "end": 683.65, "mood": "court", "scene": "court"}, {"i": 33, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Tomorrow we go to the head of the table. Nine crowns, nine seals in gold, and the manners the book expects you to show them. The fifth night is called The Kings.", "start": 684.4, "end": 697.55}, {"i": 34, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Until then. Mind the stairs on your way up. And if, in that grey hour before sunrise, you pass an old man on a pale horse, do nod to him. He has been waiting a very long time for a promotion.", "start": 698.3, "end": 711.2}], "glossary": [{"id": "president", "term": "President", "def": "One of the seven ranks of the Goetia. The word meant one who presides, such as the head of a council or college; the book gives presidents seals of mercury."}, {"id": "planetary-metals", "term": "Planetary metals", "def": "The old pairing of seven metals with the seven classical planets: gold with the Sun, silver with the Moon, copper with Venus, tin with Jupiter, lead with Saturn, mercury with Mercury and iron with Mars. The Goetia assigns each rank's seal a metal."}, {"id": "legion", "term": "Legion", "def": "The unit in which the book counts each spirit's followers, borrowed from the Roman army. The Goetia never gives its size; Weyer and Scot put it at six thousand six hundred and sixty-six."}, {"id": "four-kings", "term": "Kings of the Quarters", "def": "Four great kings set over the seventy-two, one for each cardinal direction. The Goetia names them Amaymon, Corson, Ziminiar and Goap; other grimoires use different names."}, {"id": "familiar", "term": "Familiar", "def": "An attendant spirit said to serve a magician. Several spirits of the Goetia are described as giving good familiars."}]}, {"id": "ep05", "num": 5, "title": "The Kings", "subtitle": "Nine crowns and the manners of Hell", "blurb": "A royal procession: the king on the camel whose roar cannot be understood, the monster you must take your hat off to, and the beautiful one who lies.", "mood": "court", "scene": "court", "audio": "audio/ep05.m4a", "duration": 718.7, "words": 1833, "segments": [{"i": 0, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Listen. Before anything comes into view, there is a sound. Trumpets, a long way off. Then cymbals, bright and badly behaved. Then, underneath both, something that might be a voice, if a voice could be the size of a hillside. That is how you know a king of Hell is coming. You hear him long before you see him.", "start": 2.5, "end": 26.3}, {"i": 1, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Last night we walked the courts. Ranks, metals, legions. Tonight we stand still and let the top of it come to us. Nine spirits in this book carry the title of king. Their seals are meant to be made in gold, the metal of the sun. So think of tonight as a procession. You are at the edge of the circle. Stay behind the line, and watch them pass.", "start": 27.05, "end": 51.7}, {"i": 2, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Two of the nine you know already. Bael came through on the first night, hoarse, wearing a cat and a toad and a man all at once. Beleth rode past on the third, on a pale horse, in a fury, with his own band playing. Nod to them. They will not nod back. Now look west. The noise is getting louder, and the one making it is the most famous king in the book.", "start": 52.45, "end": 78.85}, {"i": 3, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "The Ninth Spirit in this Order is Paimon, a Great King, and very obedient unto Lucifer. He appeareth in the form of a Man sitting upon a Dromedary with a Crown most glorious upon his head.", "start": 79.6, "end": 92.15, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 9"}, {"i": 4, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Paimon. A king, and a great one. Two hundred legions follow him, the largest army in the whole book. He comes as a man on a dromedary, which is a camel with one hump, the fast kind. And ahead of him walks a host of spirits shaped like men, with trumpets, cymbals and every sort of instrument. A king who travels with his own orchestra. His seal is in your grimoire now.", "start": 93.2, "end": 119.45, "spirit": 9, "term": "dromedary"}, {"i": 5, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He hath a great Voice, and roareth at his first coming, and his speech is such that the Magician cannot well understand unless he can compel him.", "start": 121.1, "end": 130.45, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 9", "mood": "storm", "scene": "storm"}, {"i": 6, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "I think that is the best stage direction in the book. He arrives roaring, in a voice so large that the words will not come apart into sense. The magician has to compel him to speak plainly. Until then it is only weather. All that music, that crown, that patient camel, and a king shouting something enormous at you that you cannot follow.", "start": 131.5, "end": 154.65}, {"i": 7, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And once he is made plain, what does he give? He teaches all arts and sciences, and secret things. He gives dignity, meaning rank, and confirms it. He can bind any man to the magician's will. And he gives good familiars, which are lesser spirits lent out as servants or tutors. He is to be looked for toward the west. Then the book makes a promise.", "start": 155.4, "end": 180.2, "term": "familiar"}, {"i": 8, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He can discover unto thee what the Earth is, and what holdeth it up in the Waters; and what Mind is, and where it is; or any other thing thou mayest desire to know.", "start": 180.95, "end": 192.35, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 9"}, {"i": 9, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "What the earth is, and what holds it up. What mind is, and where it is. I find it touching that a book of demons saves its grandest king for the questions a curious child would ask first. Paimon is the best known of the nine today, well beyond readers of old books. Why that happened is a story for the eleventh night.", "start": 193.4, "end": 214.35}, {"i": 10, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep05-t1", "v": "keeper", "t": "While he passes, let me ask you something. Paimon will tell you one thing, plainly, with the roar turned off. What do you ask him?", "start": 215.1, "end": 223.7, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "What holds the earth up", "trait": "scholar", "reply": "The practical one. In the sixteen hundreds that was a live question, and the honest answer was that nobody in the room knew. We would tell you now that nothing holds it up. It falls forever around the sun. I suspect Paimon would enjoy saying that to a man standing in a chalk circle.", "audio": "audio/ep05-t1-a.m4a", "dur": 18.4}, {"key": "b", "label": "Where the mind is", "trait": "poet", "reply": "Ah. You want the hard one. Four centuries on, with machines that can watch a brain at work, people still argue about what a mind even is. If the king on the camel knows, he has kept it to himself. I would ask the same, and I would not expect a comfortable answer.", "audio": "audio/ep05-t1-b.m4a", "dur": 21.2}, {"key": "c", "label": "Why all the trumpets", "trait": "skeptic", "reply": "A fair question, and a sharp one. The trumpets are there because a king without a noise is only a man on a camel. Every court on earth has known that. The people who imagined Hell could not imagine power arriving quietly. That tells you a good deal about power, and nothing whatever about Hell.", "audio": "audio/ep05-t1-c.m4a", "dur": 18.6}]}, {"i": 11, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Keep your question. The music is fading west, and the next king is already at the line. Fewer trumpets, more teeth. He also comes with the strangest rule of manners in the whole book, so stand up straight.", "start": 225.6, "end": 239.25, "mood": "fire", "scene": "fire"}, {"i": 12, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Asmoday. A king, with seventy-two legions. He has three heads. One of a bull, one of a man, one of a ram. He has a serpent's tail, and feet webbed like a goose, and flames come out of his mouth. He sits on an infernal dragon, and in his hand he carries a lance with a banner on it, like a knight at a tournament. The book calls him first and choicest under Amaymon, one of the great kings of the quarters from last night.", "start": 240.0, "end": 268.3, "spirit": 32}, {"i": 13, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Now the rule. The book says that whoever calls him must stand on his feet the whole time, with his cap or headdress off. Bareheaded. If the cap stays on, Amaymon will deceive him and all his work will be betrayed. The book's word for the magician is exorcist, which in these pages means the one who commands a spirit. So. Three heads and goose feet are riding a dragon toward you. And the instruction is, take off your hat.", "start": 269.05, "end": 295.45, "term": "exorcist"}, {"i": 14, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "But as soon as the Exorcist seeth Asmoday in the shape aforesaid, he shall call him by his Name, saying: Art thou Asmoday? and he will not deny it, and by-and-by he will bow down unto the ground.", "start": 296.2, "end": 309.95, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 32"}, {"i": 15, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "That is an introduction at court. You uncover your head, you ask his name, he owns it, he bows. Then come the gifts. He gives something called the Ring of Virtues. He teaches arithmetic, astronomy, geometry and every handicraft. He answers truly and fully. He makes a person invincible, and he shows where treasure lies, and guards it. His name is far older than this book. That story is waiting on the ninth night.", "start": 311.0, "end": 340.7}, {"i": 16, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Now the procession quickens. Four kings, close together, and you will notice they share a wardrobe. First, Purson. A king, with twenty-two legions. A comely man with the face of a lion, riding a bear, holding a cruel viper in his hand, and many trumpets sounding before him. He knows all hidden things. He finds treasure. He tells past, present and future. He answers truly about earthly things, secret and divine, and about the creation of the world, and he brings good familiars. A lion's face on a bear's back, and what he sells is an honest answer.", "start": 342.35, "end": 383.45, "spirit": 20, "mood": "court", "scene": "court"}, {"i": 17, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Behind him, Vine. A king and also an earl, with thirty-six legions. He comes as a lion riding a black horse, with a viper in his hand. Two lions and two vipers in a row. Vine discovers hidden things, and witches and wizards, and things present, past and to come. He is also the engineer of the nine.", "start": 384.2, "end": 405.25, "spirit": 45}, {"i": 18, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He, at the command of the Exorcist will build Towers, overthrow Great Stone Walls, and make the Waters rough with Storms.", "start": 406.0, "end": 413.9, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 45"}, {"i": 19, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Towers up, walls down, storms on the water. Then Balam, a king with forty legions. Three heads again, a bull, a man and a ram, the same three as Asmoday. A serpent's tail. Flaming eyes. He rides a furious bear, carries a goshawk on his fist like a gentleman out hunting, and speaks with a hoarse voice. He gives true answers about things past, present and to come, and he makes men invisible, and witty.", "start": 414.95, "end": 446.15, "spirit": 51}, {"i": 20, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And last of the four, Zagan. A king and a president, with thirty-three legions. He comes first as a bull with the wings of a griffin, and after a while he puts on a human shape. He makes men witty. Then he does a trick that would have made a reader in the sixteen hundreds sit very still, because they knew exactly whose miracle it was.", "start": 446.9, "end": 471.25, "spirit": 61}, {"i": 21, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He can turn Wine into Water, and Blood into Wine, also Water into Wine. He can turn all Metals into Coin of the Dominion that Metal is of. He can even make Fools wise.", "start": 472.0, "end": 484.0, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 61"}, {"i": 22, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Water into wine, and wine back into water, which seems unkind. Metal into the coin of the realm, which is thoughtful. And then the quiet one at the end. He can even make fools wise. Of everything on offer tonight, that may be the one miracle nobody has ever managed to buy.", "start": 485.05, "end": 506.45}, {"i": 23, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Step back from the line a moment and count the gifts. Knowledge. Hidden things found. True answers. Wit. Dignities, which are ranks and offices. Almost nobody tonight has offered to hurt anyone. This is my reading, not the manuscripts. I think the list is a mirror. It shows what a clever, obscure person in the sixteen hundreds wanted and could not get. An education. A patron. A place at court.", "start": 507.2, "end": 535.0, "term": "dignity"}, {"i": 24, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep05-t2", "v": "keeper", "t": "So let me hold that mirror up to you. One royal gift, tonight only, no questions asked. Which do you take?", "start": 535.75, "end": 543.9, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "A place at court", "trait": "seeker", "reply": "Rank, then. A seat at the table and a door that opens when you knock. Most of the people who copied this book would have chosen the same, and they would have been ashamed to say so. You are not. Good. Wait for the last king before you sign anything.", "audio": "audio/ep05-t2-a.m4a", "dur": 16.7}, {"key": "b", "label": "The coin, thank you", "trait": "skeptic", "reply": "Sensible. Money is the one gift on the list you could check with your teeth. I notice Zagan pays in local currency, which suggests whoever wrote his entry had been cheated by a moneychanger at least once. Take the coin. Count it twice.", "audio": "audio/ep05-t2-b.m4a", "dur": 16.8}, {"key": "c", "label": "Make the fools wise", "trait": "poet", "reply": "The generous answer. I note you did not say which fools. That is the charm of it. Everyone who asks Zagan for this gift has somebody else in mind, and the book, very tactfully, does not say where he begins. I hope it works. I hope it is painless.", "audio": "audio/ep05-t2-c.m4a", "dur": 17.5}]}, {"i": 25, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Hold on to it. There is one king left, and you may want to count your change after him. The trumpets have stopped. He rides no animal. He comes in fire, and he is the most beautiful thing in the procession.", "start": 545.8, "end": 563.8, "mood": "fire", "scene": "fire"}, {"i": 26, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He appeareth in the Form of Two Beautiful Angels sitting in a Chariot of Fire. He speaketh with a Comely Voice, and declareth that he fell first from among the worthier sort, that were before Michael, and other Heavenly Angels.", "start": 564.55, "end": 579.75, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 68"}, {"i": 27, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Belial. A mighty king, created, the book says, next after Lucifer. He appears as two beautiful angels sitting in a chariot of fire, and he speaks with a comely voice. He commands eighty legions in the text I am following. The printings disagree, and some say fifty. Even his soldiers will not hold still.", "start": 580.8, "end": 602.3, "spirit": 68}, {"i": 28, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "You have met him before. On the first night, when the brass vessel was broken open and the spirits flew home, one did not go. Belial slipped into a statue and answered the people who sacrificed to it. Now look at what he gives. Presentations and senatorships. A presentation was the right to name a priest to a paid post in a church. A senatorship is a seat in government. He hands out jobs. He wins you the favour of friends and of foes, and he gives excellent familiars.", "start": 603.05, "end": 633.35, "term": "presentation"}, {"i": 29, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Here is the catch, and it is a courtier's catch. Belial must be given offerings, sacrifices and gifts, or he will not answer truly at all. And even paid, the book warns, he does not stay one hour in the truth unless divine power holds him to it. So the most beautiful king, the one with the best jobs to give, has to be bribed, and lies anyway. Whoever wrote that had been to court.", "start": 634.1, "end": 657.6}, {"i": 30, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "That is the procession. Nine crowns, and you have seen them all. Notice how courtly it was. Hats off. Names asked and given. Bows, banners, bands, presents for the great man. The people who copied this book could picture Hell quite easily. It looked like the palace they were never invited into.", "start": 659.25, "end": 680.65, "mood": "court", "scene": "court"}, {"i": 31, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Tomorrow we leave the throne room for somewhere quieter. A surprising number of the spirits in this book are teachers. They offer philosophy, logic, medicine, the languages of birds. We will call the night The Faculty, and I will show you a Hell that looks very like a university.", "start": 682.3, "end": 700.8, "mood": "library", "scene": "library"}, {"i": 32, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Until then. The kings have gone by, and seven new seals are in your grimoire. And if you find yourself in grand company this week, remember the rule. Stand up. Cap off. Ask the name.", "start": 701.55, "end": 714.9}], "glossary": [{"id": "dromedary", "term": "Dromedary", "def": "A one-humped camel, the swift riding kind. Paimon's mount in the Goetia, and the reason he is so easy to recognise in old illustrations."}, {"id": "familiar", "term": "Familiar", "def": "An attendant spirit said to serve a magician. Several spirits of the Goetia are described as giving good familiars."}, {"id": "exorcist", "term": "Exorcist", "def": "In the Goetia, simply the magician: the person who calls and commands a spirit. The modern meaning, someone who drives a spirit out, came to dominate later."}, {"id": "dignity", "term": "Dignities", "def": "Ranks, titles and offices. When the book says a spirit gives dignities, it means worldly promotion: a post, a title, a place at court."}, {"id": "presentation", "term": "Presentations and Senatorships", "def": "Belial's gifts. A presentation was the right to appoint a clergyman to a paid church living; a senatorship is a seat in government. In plain terms, good jobs."}]}, {"id": "ep06", "num": 6, "title": "The Faculty", "subtitle": "The night school where the lecturers have wings", "blurb": "Count what the seventy-two actually offer and the commonest gift is not gold or murder but an education: a walk through Hell's night school, and the one teacher who will not lie.", "mood": "library", "scene": "library", "audio": "audio/ep06.m4a", "duration": 799.4, "words": 2016, "segments": [{"i": 0, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Last night was all crowns and trumpets. Tonight is quieter. Tonight I want you to do a small sum with me. Take the seventy-two spirits, one at a time, and ask each of them the only question a customer cares about. What do you actually do?", "start": 2.5, "end": 18.95}, {"i": 1, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "You would expect gold. You would expect love, and revenge, and a neighbour's cattle dropping dead in the field. They are in here. But count them. About a dozen spirits deal in love. A few more find treasure. Fewer than ten are much use for killing anybody. And then there is the largest group, by a distance. Count generously and you pass thirty. They teach.", "start": 19.7, "end": 45.95}, {"i": 2, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Astronomy. Logic. Geometry. Rhetoric, which is the art of speaking well. The properties of herbs. Hell, in this book, is largely a university. So tonight is enrolment. Twelve of the staff are waiting. Bring your grimoire. The first door on the left is the school of medicine.", "start": 46.7, "end": 65.75}, {"i": 3, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "The fifth spirit is Marbas. He is a great president, governing thirty-six legions. President is a middling rank, you may remember, with seals made in mercury, the metal of the planet of scholars. Half of tonight's faculty hold it. Marbas arrives as a great lion, and only puts on human shape if the master asks him to. Here is his course description.", "start": 66.5, "end": 91.7, "spirit": 5}, {"i": 4, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He answereth truly of things Hidden or Secret. He causeth Diseases and cureth them. Again, he giveth great Wisdom and Knowledge in Mechanical Arts; and can change men into other shapes.", "start": 92.45, "end": 106.9, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 5"}, {"i": 5, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "He causes diseases and cures them. In that order. I do not think that is a slip. Anyone who knew a physician four hundred years ago understood it perfectly. The person who knows how a body is mended is the person who knows how it is broken. Your doctor is a lion, and he could go either way.", "start": 107.95, "end": 128.5}, {"i": 6, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Across the corridor is his colleague. Buer, the tenth spirit, a great president over fifty legions. He teaches philosophy, both moral and natural, which is to say ethics and what we would now call science. He teaches logic, and the virtues of all herbs and plants. He heals all distempers in man. And he gives good familiars. A familiar is a small attendant spirit that stays with you. Think of a research assistant you can never dismiss.", "start": 129.25, "end": 158.25, "spirit": 10, "term": "familiar"}, {"i": 7, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "If you have ever seen a picture of Buer, you saw a lion's head with five goat legs spinning around it like a wheel. Wonderful, and not in this book. That engraving was made in Paris in eighteen sixty-three, for a French dictionary of demons, long after our manuscripts. The Lesser Key says only that he appears in Sagittary, the sign of the Archer, and that this is his shape when the Sun is there.", "start": 159.0, "end": 183.5, "term": "dictionnaire-infernal"}, {"i": 8, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Up the stairs now, and out onto the roof, into the cold. This is the observatory, and it belongs to Stolas. The thirty-sixth spirit, with twenty-six legions. Let the book introduce him.", "start": 185.15, "end": 199.1, "spirit": 36, "mood": "void", "scene": "void"}, {"i": 9, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He is a Great and Powerful Prince, appearing in the Shape of a Mighty Raven at first before the Exorcist; but after he taketh the image of a Man. He teacheth the Art of Astronomy, and the Virtues of Herbs and Precious Stones.", "start": 199.85, "end": 215.35, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 36"}, {"i": 10, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "A raven. You may have been promised an owl. The owl is real, but he is later. The same Paris engraver drew Stolas as a crowned owl on long thin legs, and that is the picture the world kept. In our book he is a mighty raven who becomes a man, and he teaches three things. Stars, herbs, stones. That is the entire entry. No treasure, no threats. Just the syllabus.", "start": 216.4, "end": 244.25}, {"i": 11, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And those three belong together. To a scholar of that age, a star, a plant and a gem were chapters of one subject. Each planet was thought to pour its influence down into particular herbs and particular stones. That hidden power was called a virtue. Know the sky, and you know which leaf to pick, and when. So Stolas is teaching one course, not three, from the roof, at night, to anybody who asks.", "start": 245.0, "end": 271.4, "term": "virtues"}, {"i": 12, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Two more work in this department. Foras, the thirty-first spirit, a mighty president with twenty-nine legions, appears as a strong man in human shape. No wings. Just a man. He teaches the virtues of herbs and precious stones, and logic and ethics in all their parts. If you ask, he makes men invisible, long-lived and eloquent. He also recovers things lost. Every school needs one of those.", "start": 272.15, "end": 300.05, "spirit": 31}, {"i": 13, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And Amy, the fifty-eighth, a great president with thirty-six legions. He comes in first as a flaming fire, and after a while, the book says, he puts on the shape of a man. He makes you wonderfully knowing in astrology and all the liberal sciences. Those are the seven subjects of a medieval degree. Grammar, logic, rhetoric, arithmetic, geometry, music, astronomy. Picture a lecture hall lit entirely by its own lecturer.", "start": 300.8, "end": 330.0, "spirit": 58, "term": "liberal-arts"}, {"i": 14, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep06-t1", "v": "keeper", "t": "Stop on the landing a moment. Term has begun, and you may sign up for one class only. Which is it?", "start": 330.75, "end": 337.3, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "Medicine, with the lion", "trait": "seeker", "reply": "Brave. You have chosen the teacher who can make you ill in order to demonstrate the cure. But I understand it. Of everything on offer tonight, healing is the one gift a person might want for somebody else. Sit near the door, all the same. Lions do not always wait for the end of the lecture.", "audio": "audio/ep06-t1-a.m4a", "dur": 20.1}, {"key": "b", "label": "The stars, with the raven", "trait": "poet", "reply": "Of course. Most people choose the roof. There is something about a teacher who waits until dark, and then points upward, and expects nothing but your attention. You will not get rich in that class. You will come down the stairs knowing the names of things, which is a different kind of wealth.", "audio": "audio/ep06-t1-b.m4a", "dur": 20.6}, {"key": "c", "label": "Logic, with the plain man", "trait": "skeptic", "reply": "Sensible. No fire, no feathers. Just a strong man in human shape, teaching you how an argument holds together. And notice what you have done. You walked into a school staffed by demons and asked for the one subject that would help you doubt it. Foras would approve. He teaches ethics as well.", "audio": "audio/ep06-t1-c.m4a", "dur": 21.7}]}, {"i": 15, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Then take your seat later, because there is a noise coming from the yard. That is the language faculty. They teach outside, since not all of their subjects are human. Barbatos is the eighth spirit, a great duke with thirty legions. Like Buer, he comes when the Sun is in Sagittary. Unlike Buer, he does not come alone. Four noble kings ride with him, and their companies of great troops. All that pageantry. And this is what he offers.", "start": 339.2, "end": 366.75, "spirit": 8, "mood": "storm", "scene": "storm"}, {"i": 16, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He giveth understanding of the singing of Birds, and of the Voices of other creatures, such as the barking of Dogs.", "start": 367.5, "end": 375.65, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 8"}, {"i": 17, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "The barking of dogs. I find that almost unbearably sweet. Somebody, four centuries ago, wanted to know what the dog was saying, badly enough to ask a duke of Hell. Barbatos also breaks open enchanted treasure, knows things past and to come, and reconciles friends. But it is the birds everyone remembers.", "start": 376.7, "end": 396.95}, {"i": 18, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "His colleague is the debater. Camio, the fifty-third spirit, a great president with thirty legions. He arrives as a thrush, a small brown songbird. Then he is a man carrying a sharp sword. And he seems to answer, the book says, in burning ashes, or in coals of fire. He is a good disputer, meaning he argues well. He gives the understanding of birds, of lowing bullocks, of barking dogs, and of the voice of the waters. He will teach you what the river is saying.", "start": 398.6, "end": 429.1, "spirit": 53, "mood": "fire", "scene": "fire"}, {"i": 19, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Now the grand rooms at the front of the building. This is the faculty of getting on in the world. Naberius, the twenty-fourth spirit, a most valiant marquis with nineteen legions, is a black crane who flutters about the circle and speaks with a hoarse voice. He makes men cunning in all arts and sciences, but especially in rhetoric. And he restores lost dignities and honours. A hoarse bird who teaches public speaking. The book does not appear to notice the joke.", "start": 430.75, "end": 462.6, "spirit": 24, "mood": "court", "scene": "court"}, {"i": 20, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Next door is Gusion, the eleventh spirit, a great and strong duke with forty legions. He appears, the book says, like a Xenopilus. Nobody knows what a Xenopilus is. Whatever he looks like, he tells all things past, present and to come, resolves any question you put to him, mends friendships, and gives honour and dignity. The examiner, I think. The one who already knows your answer.", "start": 463.35, "end": 490.2, "spirit": 11}, {"i": 21, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Vapula, the sixtieth, is a great and mighty duke with thirty-six legions, and he is a lion with gryphon's wings. He makes men knowing in all handicrafts and professions, and in philosophy and other sciences too. The workshop and the seminar, in one room, and the book does not rank them.", "start": 490.95, "end": 509.0, "spirit": 60}, {"i": 22, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And Haagenti, the forty-eighth, a president with thirty-three legions. A mighty bull, with the same gryphon's wings, until he is commanded into human shape. He makes men wise. He turns all metals into gold, which is alchemy, the one science every poor student hoped was real. And he changes wine into water, and water into wine. One direction is a famous miracle. The other is what a dishonest innkeeper does. The book offers both, without comment.", "start": 509.75, "end": 539.0, "spirit": 48}, {"i": 23, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Before you sign anything, read the small print. This is still a catalogue of fallen spirits, and the book never lets you forget it. Scattered through the entries are warnings. This one deceives. That one answers truly only when compelled. There is an earl called Furfur, whom you will meet tomorrow night, and the book says he never speaks truth unless he is brought inside the triangle. Imagine a lecturer who is brilliant, patient, and sometimes wrong on purpose.", "start": 539.75, "end": 567.95}, {"i": 24, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Which is why the last office on the corridor matters. Orobas, the fifty-fifth spirit, a great and mighty prince with twenty legions. He appears first like a horse, and at the command of the exorcist, which is the book's word for whoever is doing the calling, he puts on the image of a man. He answers truly about divinity and the creation of the world. He gives dignities and prelacies, a prelacy being high office in the church, which tells you something about who was asking. And then this.", "start": 569.6, "end": 599.3, "spirit": 55, "mood": "dawn", "scene": "dawn"}, {"i": 25, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He is very faithful unto the Exorcist, and will not suffer him to be tempted of any Spirit.", "start": 600.05, "end": 606.05, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 55"}, {"i": 26, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Faithful. In a list of seventy-two, you learn to hear how rare that word is. He will not let the others work on you. Stolas has the stars, and Buer has the medicine. Orobas has something odder. He is on your side. Readers have singled him out for centuries as the honest one. The horse who stands between you and the rest of the faculty.", "start": 607.1, "end": 630.6}, {"i": 27, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep06-t2", "v": "keeper", "t": "So choose your tutor. One knows more than anyone in the building, but may lie to you. The other knows less, and never will. Who gets your evening?", "start": 631.35, "end": 641.1, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "The honest horse", "trait": "seeker", "reply": "Then you have chosen the way most readers of this book have chosen. Orobas is quietly one of its best loved names, and not for his power. A smaller truth you can lean your whole weight on is worth a great deal. Especially at night. Especially in a building like this one.", "audio": "audio/ep06-t2-a.m4a", "dur": 18.5}, {"key": "b", "label": "The brilliant liar", "trait": "scholar", "reply": "Then you will have to check everything he tells you. Against other books, other teachers, your own eyes. Which, I should point out, is simply what scholarship is. You may be the only student here who ends up with a real education. I would still not lend him money.", "audio": "audio/ep06-t2-b.m4a", "dur": 16.5}, {"key": "c", "label": "Neither. I'll read alone", "trait": "skeptic", "reply": "A reasonable answer, and a lonely one. Remember, though, that the people who wrote this book would have given anything for your choice. You have a library in your pocket. They had one borrowed book, a candle end, and a strong suspicion that everybody else had been told something they had not.", "audio": "audio/ep06-t2-c.m4a", "dur": 20.3}]}, {"i": 28, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Keep that answer. Now ask the better question. Why is a book of demons shaped like a college prospectus? Look at who copied it. Not kings. Not witches in the woods. The historian Richard Kieckhefer gave them a name. The clerical underworld. Clerks. Students. Minor clergy. Men with a little Latin, a little ritual, and not much else. Men who had stood close enough to learning to know exactly what they were missing.", "start": 643.0, "end": 672.9, "mood": "library", "scene": "library", "term": "clerical-underworld"}, {"i": 29, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Think what learning cost them. A single book could be worth months of wages. A university took years, and money, and the right friends. And every one of them knew somebody duller who had risen further, because he could talk well in front of a bishop. Hence rhetoric. Hence all those lost dignities, restored.", "start": 673.65, "end": 695.6}, {"i": 30, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "So their daydream of power was not a throne. It was a tutor. Someone who would appear in the room at night and pour an entire science into your head before morning. On the second night we looked at the last book in this collection, the Ars Notoria. It has no demons in it at all. Only prayers, promising memory, eloquence and the liberal arts in a fraction of the usual time. The Goetia is the same hunger, in worse company.", "start": 696.35, "end": 725.6, "term": "ars-notoria"}, {"i": 31, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And that is the part I find tender. Read past the lions and the fire, and these twelve are the teachers nobody had. Patient. Awake at any hour. Willing to explain the stars to a nobody. Somebody lonely and clever imagined a raven who would sit with him and teach him the sky. And the same hand wrote down that the staff might lie. He wanted a teacher, and he did not trust his luck. Both things are true at once. That is the faculty.", "start": 726.35, "end": 758.35}, {"i": 32, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Twelve seals are in your grimoire now. Tomorrow the tone drops. If tonight was what clever poor men wanted for their minds, tomorrow is what they wanted for everything else. Love they had not earned. Money that was not theirs. The seventh night is called Lovers, Liars and Thieves. You will not need a pencil.", "start": 759.1, "end": 779.9}, {"i": 33, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Until then. Class dismissed. Leave by the main door. If a horse offers to walk you home, say yes. If anything else offers, thank it kindly, and ask it to step into the triangle first.", "start": 780.65, "end": 795.65}], "glossary": [{"id": "familiar", "term": "Familiar", "def": "An attendant spirit said to serve a magician. Several spirits of the Goetia are described as giving good familiars."}, {"id": "dictionnaire-infernal", "term": "Dictionnaire Infernal", "def": "A French dictionary of demons by Collin de Plancy. Its illustrated edition of eighteen sixty-three, with engravings after Louis Le Breton, gave many Goetic spirits the faces people know today, including the wheel-legged Buer and the owl Stolas. Those pictures are not in the Lesser Key."}, {"id": "virtues", "term": "Virtues of herbs and stones", "def": "In older natural philosophy, the hidden powers of a plant or gem, often thought to flow down from a particular planet. Not moral virtue: closer to our word properties."}, {"id": "liberal-arts", "term": "Liberal arts", "def": "The seven subjects of a medieval education: grammar, logic and rhetoric, then arithmetic, geometry, music and astronomy. The Goetia also calls them the liberal sciences."}, {"id": "clerical-underworld", "term": "Clerical underworld", "def": "The historian Richard Kieckhefer's phrase for the clerks, students and minor clergy who had enough Latin and ritual training to copy and use books of spirit magic in the late Middle Ages."}, {"id": "ars-notoria", "term": "Ars Notoria", "def": "The notary art: medieval prayers, in circulation by the thirteenth century, that promise rapid learning and a perfect memory. It became the fifth book of the Lesser Key, though some manuscripts omit it."}]}, {"id": "ep07", "num": 7, "title": "Lovers, Liars and Thieves", "subtitle": "What people really asked the dark for", "blurb": "Love, money and revenge: twelve spirits for the wishes nobody admits to, and the one entry where the book warns that the demon may kill you.", "mood": "fire", "scene": "fire", "audio": "audio/ep07.m4a", "duration": 796.6, "words": 2026, "segments": [{"i": 0, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Nobody ever lit a candle at midnight to ask a demon for geometry lessons. Well. A few did. You met their teachers last night, in The Faculty. Astronomy, logic, the virtues of herbs. That was the respectable half of the book. The half you could explain to your mother.", "start": 2.5, "end": 21.7}, {"i": 1, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Tonight is the other half. Tonight is what people actually whispered for. Make her love me. Make me rich. Make him pay. And let me get away with it. This is the night the book stops being a university and becomes a confession. Twelve spirits. Lovers first, then thieves, then blades. Come closer to the fire.", "start": 22.45, "end": 43.35}, {"i": 2, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "We start with love, because love magic is the oldest trade there is. There are love charms scratched on lead tablets in ancient Greece, and written on papyrus in Roman Egypt, a thousand years and more before this book. The Goetia simply gives the old job a title and a staff.", "start": 44.1, "end": 64.8, "term": "love-magic"}, {"i": 3, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "The head of the department is a prince. Sitri. Sixty legions. He arrives with a leopard's head and the wings of a gryphon, which is an eagle crossed with a lion. Then, when the magician asks, he puts on a human shape, and the book takes care to add, a very beautiful one. Here is his office.", "start": 65.55, "end": 86.3, "spirit": 12}, {"i": 4, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He enflameth men with Women's love, and Women with Men's love; and causeth them also to show themselves naked if it be desired.", "start": 87.05, "end": 96.9, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 12"}, {"i": 5, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "If it be desired. Four small words doing a great deal of work. Desired by whom? Not, you will notice, by the person undressing. Keep that question in your pocket. We will need it in a minute.", "start": 97.95, "end": 111.65}, {"i": 6, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Next, a duke in red. Zepar. Twenty-six legions. He comes as a soldier, in red clothes and red armour, and his office is to make women love men and bring them together. Then the entry adds one short sentence and no explanation. He also makes them barren. Cruelty or convenience, the book does not say.", "start": 112.4, "end": 135.55, "spirit": 16}, {"i": 7, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He is a Great and Mighty Duke, and appeareth in the form of a gallant Soldier riding on a Crocodile, with a Ducal Crown on his head, but peaceably.", "start": 136.3, "end": 147.1, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 19"}, {"i": 8, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Sallos. A duke with thirty legions, a crown, a crocodile for a horse, and that lovely last word. Peaceably. He causes the love of women to men and of men to women, and that is all he does. No storms. No treasure. No lies. In a book full of sharp edges, Sallos reads like a wedding guest. I have a soft spot for him.", "start": 148.15, "end": 171.7, "spirit": 19}, {"i": 9, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Then two who come by camel. Uvall is a duke with thirty-seven legions, and he appears first as a mighty dromedary, the camel with one hump. On command he becomes a man and speaks the Egyptian tongue, the book says, but not perfectly. A demon with an accent. He procures the love of women, tells past and future, and makes friends of enemies. In nineteen oh four the editor added a footnote, saying Uvall can nowadays manage sound though colloquial Coptic. That is a joke. The book has very few.", "start": 172.45, "end": 206.4, "spirit": 47}, {"i": 10, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He is a Duke Strong and Powerful, and appeareth in the Form of a Beautiful Woman, with a Duchess's Crown tied about her waist, and riding on a Great Camel.", "start": 207.15, "end": 216.9, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 56"}, {"i": 11, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Gremory. Twenty-six legions. Listen to the grammar. He is a duke. He appears as a beautiful woman. The crown is a duchess's crown, and it is tied about her waist, not set on her head, and nobody explains why. Gremory tells past, present and future, finds hidden treasure, and procures the love of women, both young and old. One of the very few spirits who arrives as a woman, and the book still says he.", "start": 217.95, "end": 246.0, "spirit": 56}, {"i": 12, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Now take that question out of your pocket. Read these entries in a row and notice who is doing the wanting. To procure the love of women. To cause women to love men. Women appear here the way treasure does, as something to be found and fetched. Only a couple of entries trouble to say it runs both ways. That tells you who held the pen. Men. Latin-reading men, many of them clerics and students, often poor, often unmarried. The clerical underworld, as you heard last night. These are not the spells of the powerful. They are the daydreams of lonely men with a library.", "start": 246.75, "end": 283.65}, {"i": 13, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "One more lover, and he brings the weather. Furfur. An earl, twenty-six legions. He appears as a hart, which is a stag, with a tail of fire. He raises lightning, thunder and great storms, and he urges love between man and woman. Passion and thunder in one office. But Furfur comes with a warning label.", "start": 285.3, "end": 307.4, "spirit": 34, "mood": "storm", "scene": "storm"}, {"i": 14, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He never speaketh truth unless he be compelled, or brought up within a triangle. Being therein, he will take upon himself the Form of an Angel.", "start": 308.15, "end": 317.75, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 34"}, {"i": 15, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "The triangle. You saw it on the third night. A triangle drawn outside the magician's circle, the place where the spirit is told to stand. Inside it, the book claims, a spirit must tell the truth. Outside it, Furfur lies about everything. Put him in, and the burning stag becomes an angel with a hoarse voice, and answers truly. So the book's great spirit of married love cannot be trusted on any subject until he is cornered. I leave you to draw your own conclusions.", "start": 318.8, "end": 349.65, "term": "triangle-of-art"}, {"i": 16, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep07-t1", "v": "keeper", "t": "So the lovers are all in the room. A leopard prince, a soldier in red, a gentleman on a crocodile, two camels and a burning stag. If one of them had to carry a message for you, who goes?", "start": 350.4, "end": 365.45, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "Sallos, peaceably", "trait": "poet", "reply": "The crocodile. Good. You picked the only one whose entry contains no threat, no trick and no small print, just a crown and a quiet manner. People who choose Sallos usually do not want to win anyone. They want to be met halfway. That is a different wish, and a better one.", "audio": "audio/ep07-t1-a.m4a", "dur": 17.8}, {"key": "b", "label": "Furfur, in the triangle", "trait": "seeker", "reply": "The stag with the fiery tail. You want the thunder with it, then. Fair enough. But notice you put him in the triangle before you sent him. You already know that anything which arrives with lightning should be asked twice whether it is telling the truth. Hold on to that instinct.", "audio": "audio/ep07-t1-b.m4a", "dur": 19.2}, {"key": "c", "label": "None. I'll ask myself", "trait": "skeptic", "reply": "The hardest spell in the book, and the only one I can promise has ever worked. Walk across the room and say it. I suspect half the men who copied these pages would have given all sixty of Sitri's legions for the nerve to do exactly that.", "audio": "audio/ep07-t1-c.m4a", "dur": 17.4}]}, {"i": 17, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Whoever carries your message, love is only the first thing people ask for in the dark. The second is money. And the book is refreshingly direct about where money comes from. It comes from other people. Mind your pockets. We are going down to the cellar.", "start": 367.35, "end": 383.95, "mood": "crypt", "scene": "crypt"}, {"i": 18, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He is a mighty Duke, and appeareth in the shape of a Lion with an Ass's Head, bellowing. He is a good Familiar, but tempteth them he is a familiar of to steal.", "start": 384.7, "end": 394.95, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 6"}, {"i": 19, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Valefor. A duke with ten legions, a lion's body and a donkey's head, braying. A familiar is a spirit that stays, a servant at your elbow rather than a visitor you call once. And Valefor is a good one, the book insists. Loyal. Helpful. He only keeps suggesting, gently, that you steal. An older version of the list, printed in English in fifteen eighty-four, finishes the thought. He is very friendly, it says, until he has brought you to the gallows. Not a monster. A good friend with one bad idea, repeated.", "start": 396.0, "end": 431.0, "spirit": 6, "term": "familiar"}, {"i": 20, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "If Valefor is the bad influence, Raum is the professional. An earl with thirty legions arriving as a crow. His office is to steal treasure out of kings' houses and carry it wherever he is told. Note the address. Not your neighbour's house. The king's. He also destroys cities and the dignities of men, meaning their rank and good name, tells past and future, and reconciles enemies. A full service.", "start": 431.75, "end": 459.7, "spirit": 40}, {"i": 21, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And then Shax. A marquis, thirty legions, in the form of a stock dove, a plain grey wild pigeon, with a voice the book calls hoarse but subtle. Older lists make him a stork. He takes away sight, hearing or understanding. He fetches horses. He steals money out of the houses of kings and, the book says, carries it back again in twelve hundred years. The longest loan in literature. And then comes a clause you have heard before.", "start": 460.45, "end": 489.95, "spirit": 44}, {"i": 22, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "But he must first be commanded into a Triangle, or else he will deceive him, and tell him many Lies.", "start": 490.7, "end": 498.15, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 44"}, {"i": 23, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "There it is again. Furfur, now Shax, and others you will meet on other nights. The spirit lies unless constrained. I call it the book's fine print. Think about what it does. If the answer you were given turns out false, the spirit did not fail and the book did not fail. You failed, at geometry. It is a clause that makes the whole system impossible to disprove. A sceptic sees a trick. A believer sees a safety manual.", "start": 499.2, "end": 525.8}, {"i": 24, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep07-t2", "v": "keeper", "t": "Be honest with me. A helper who is clever, useful, close at hand, and known to lie. Would you take him on?", "start": 526.55, "end": 533.7, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "Yes, inside a triangle", "trait": "scholar", "reply": "The magician's answer. Use the liar, but build the room first. That is how every archive, every courtroom and every laboratory works, as it happens. Nobody trusts the witness. They trust the procedure. The men who wrote this book were, in their own strange way, inventing cross-examination.", "audio": "audio/ep07-t2-a.m4a", "dur": 21.5}, {"key": "b", "label": "Yes. I know the type", "trait": "poet", "reply": "Of course you do. We all keep a Valefor somewhere. A friend, a habit, a small voice that is excellent company and has exactly one terrible suggestion. The book's only real insight is that he does not feel like an enemy. He feels like the one who understands you.", "audio": "audio/ep07-t2-b.m4a", "dur": 20.3}, {"key": "c", "label": "No. Never", "trait": "skeptic", "reply": "Sensible. And yet consider how much of what you know reached you through sources you would not swear to. Rumour. Advertisements. Your own memory. The book's wager is that a liar you can check beats an honest silence. You may refuse the wager. Most of us take it daily.", "audio": "audio/ep07-t2-c.m4a", "dur": 19.4}]}, {"i": 25, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Love. Money. That leaves the third wish, the one nobody admits to at dinner. Revenge. Or, to give it the polite name, victory. The last three spirits tonight all come armed, and they ride in like a tournament.", "start": 535.6, "end": 551.5, "mood": "court", "scene": "court"}, {"i": 26, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Leraje. A marquis with thirty legions. He shows himself as an archer clad in green, with a bow and a quiver, like something stepped out of a ballad. It is all rather charming. Then you read what he does.", "start": 552.25, "end": 566.05, "spirit": 14}, {"i": 27, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He causeth all great Battles and Contests; and maketh wounds to putrefy that are made with Arrows by Archers.", "start": 566.8, "end": 573.9, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 14"}, {"i": 28, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Not kill. Putrefy. Whoever first wrote that had seen an arrow wound a week later, in a world without antibiotics. The wish underneath is very precise and very ugly. Do not only let my enemy lose. Let him not get better.", "start": 574.95, "end": 592.15}, {"i": 29, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Eligos is the staff officer. A duke, sixty legions, a goodly knight carrying a lance, an ensign, which is a battle flag, and a serpent. He discovers hidden things, and he knows how wars will go and how the soldiers will meet. He also causes the love of lords and great persons, which is to say, he gets you noticed by the people who hand out commands. Every captain who ever lay awake before a battle wanted Eligos. Not courage. Information.", "start": 592.9, "end": 621.6, "spirit": 15}, {"i": 30, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And then there is the sixty-third spirit. I have kept him for last. I am going to put the jokes down for a moment, and I would like you to hear his entry the way it is written. Plainly.", "start": 623.25, "end": 634.4, "mood": "void", "scene": "void"}, {"i": 31, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Andras. A marquis with thirty legions. He has the body of an angel and a head like a black night raven. He rides a strong black wolf, and he holds a sharp, bright sword flourished aloft. One word before the book speaks. Exorcist is its name for the magician, the one who commands spirits by oath. You.", "start": 635.15, "end": 658.05, "spirit": 63, "term": "exorcist"}, {"i": 32, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "His Office is to sow Discords. If the Exorcist have not a care, he will slay both him and his fellows.", "start": 658.8, "end": 666.85, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 63"}, {"i": 33, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "That is the whole entry. No knowledge. No treasure. No gift at all, unless discord is a gift. Every other spirit tonight was described by what it will do for you. Andras is described by what he will do to you. And to your fellows, the friends who came along to help. The book files him anyway, number sixty-three, with a seal, between a president and a duke, as calmly as it filed the crocodile.", "start": 667.9, "end": 695.2}, {"i": 34, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Why would anyone call him? Because someone, somewhere, wanted an enemy's house divided badly enough to stand that close to the sword. That is the far end of wanting. The book does not judge it. It notes the risk and moves on.", "start": 695.95, "end": 712.05}, {"i": 35, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "So here is what I think this night is for. Forget the demons for a moment and read the list as a list of wishes. To be loved by someone who does not love you. To have a king's money. To know how the fight ends before it starts. To watch an enemy fail to heal. It is the most honest record of ordinary human wanting you will ever find, because nobody wrote it to look good. Nobody summons a demon for what they already have.", "start": 713.7, "end": 743.1, "mood": "fire", "scene": "fire"}, {"i": 36, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Their seals are in your grimoire now, all twelve. The lovers, the thieves, the blades. Look through them later, and notice which page you linger on. I will not ask. That is between you and the book.", "start": 743.85, "end": 757.4}, {"i": 37, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Tomorrow we stop asking what they do and simply look at them. The eighth night is The Bestiary. The strangest and most beautiful shapes in the whole catalogue. Wings and scales and fire, creatures no zoo would insure, and one or two so lovely that you may forget, for a moment, what they are.", "start": 758.15, "end": 778.45}, {"i": 38, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Until then. Bank the fire. Count the spoons. And if a very good friend should suggest something to you tonight, something small and clever, sleep on it first. Good night.", "start": 779.2, "end": 792.8}], "glossary": [{"id": "love-magic", "term": "Love Magic", "def": "Charms and rites meant to make one person desire another. Among the oldest recorded kinds of magic, found on Greek lead tablets and Egyptian papyri long before the grimoires."}, {"id": "triangle-of-art", "term": "Triangle of Art", "def": "A triangle set two feet outside the circle, bearing holy names and the name of the archangel Michael. It marks the place where the summoned spirit is supposed to appear and stay."}, {"id": "familiar", "term": "Familiar", "def": "An attendant spirit said to serve a magician. Several spirits of the Goetia are described as giving good familiars."}, {"id": "exorcist", "term": "Exorcist", "def": "In the Goetia, simply the magician: the person who calls and commands a spirit. The modern meaning, someone who drives a spirit out, came to dominate later."}]}, {"id": "ep08", "num": 8, "title": "The Bestiary", "subtitle": "Sea monsters, singing birds, and three demons who want to go home", "blurb": "Forget horns and pitchforks: tonight the book sends up a mermaid, a phoenix, a peacock, a star, and a wolf who is counting the years.", "mood": "void", "scene": "void", "audio": "audio/ep08.m4a", "duration": 819.0, "words": 2062, "segments": [{"i": 0, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Close your eyes and draw me a demon. Red skin, I expect. Horns. A tail with a point on it, and a pitchfork somewhere. It is a good drawing. It is also not in the book. I have been through all seventy-two entries, and not one of them is a red man with a tail.", "start": 2.5, "end": 22.5}, {"i": 1, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Last night we kept company with lovers, liars and thieves. Tonight asks for nothing but your eyes. Because here is what the book really sends up the stairs. A sea monster. A mermaid. A phoenix that sings in a child's voice. A peacock. A star. A small boy on a dragon with two heads.", "start": 23.25, "end": 46.8}, {"i": 2, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Welcome to the eighth night. I call it the Bestiary. A bestiary was a medieval book of beasts, real and imagined, each with its picture and its meaning. Twelve spirits tonight, and for once I care less about what they do than how they arrive. Three rooms. Water, fire, and a cabinet of wonders.", "start": 47.55, "end": 71.45, "term": "bestiary"}, {"i": 3, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "We begin under water. The thirtieth spirit is Forneus, a mighty and great marquis with twenty-nine legions. The book gives his shape in four words. A great sea monster. No scales counted, no teeth measured. Only something enormous, turning over in the dark. And what does the monster do? It teaches rhetoric, the art of speaking well. And then this.", "start": 73.1, "end": 100.5, "spirit": 30, "mood": "sea", "scene": "sea"}, {"i": 4, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He causeth men to have a Good Name, and to have the knowledge and understanding of Tongues. He maketh one to be beloved of his Foes as well as of his Friends.", "start": 101.25, "end": 113.0, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 30"}, {"i": 5, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "A leviathan that gives lessons in charm. Swim up a little. The forty-second spirit is Vepar, a duke great and strong, with twenty-nine legions, and he appears like a mermaid. Yes, he. The book does not blink. Vepar governs the waters and guides ships laden with arms and armour. He has an ugly side, too. He can make a man die in three days of rotting wounds. But listen to his best trick.", "start": 114.05, "end": 145.15, "spirit": 42}, {"i": 6, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "And at the request of the Exorcist he can cause the seas to be right stormy and to appear full of ships.", "start": 145.9, "end": 152.15, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 42"}, {"i": 7, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "To appear full of ships. Not to be. A phantom navy on the horizon, sails with nothing under them. Exorcist, by the way, is only the book's word for whoever is doing the calling.", "start": 153.2, "end": 163.65}, {"i": 8, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Above the mermaid, the weather. Focalor, the forty-first spirit, a mighty duke and strong, with thirty legions. He comes as a man with a griffin's wings. A griffin is half eagle, half lion. Remember him.", "start": 164.4, "end": 179.95, "spirit": 41}, {"i": 9, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "His office is to slay Men, and to drown them in the Waters, and to overthrow Ships of War, for he hath Power over both Winds and Seas.", "start": 180.7, "end": 189.35, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 41"}, {"i": 10, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And yet the same entry says he will hurt no man or thing if he is told not to. A drowner with a safety catch. Then, almost in passing, the book adds that Focalor hopes to return to the Seventh Throne after a thousand years. Hold on to that sentence. I am coming back for it.", "start": 190.4, "end": 210.0}, {"i": 11, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "One more by the water, and the gentlest. Crocell, the forty-ninth, a duke great and strong, with forty-eight legions. He appears as an angel, speaking somewhat mystically of hidden things. He teaches geometry and the liberal sciences. He told Solomon he had belonged to the order of Powers before his fall. And he has two gifts.", "start": 210.75, "end": 231.75, "spirit": 49}, {"i": 12, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He, at the Command of the Exorcist, will produce Great Noises like the Rushings of many Waters, although there be none. He warmeth Waters, and discovereth Baths.", "start": 232.5, "end": 242.95, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 49"}, {"i": 13, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "The roar of a river in a dry room. Nothing wet, nothing moved, only the sound. A word on that order of Powers. Medieval theology sorted the angels into nine orders, like regiments, with names such as Thrones, Dominations and Powers. The book likes to note which regiment each spirit deserted.", "start": 244.0, "end": 264.5, "term": "orders"}, {"i": 14, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep08-t1", "v": "keeper", "t": "Before we leave the shore. A sea monster who teaches charm, a mermaid with a ghost fleet, an angel who sounds like a river. If one of them could rise in front of you tonight, only to be looked at, which would you choose?", "start": 265.25, "end": 280.05, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "The sea monster", "trait": "poet", "reply": "Forneus. You want the big dark shape, and I do not blame you. Notice the book never describes him, and that is why he works. Four words, and you supplied the rest yourself. Every monster you have ever loved was mostly made by you. He would approve. He teaches rhetoric, after all.", "audio": "audio/ep08-t1-a.m4a", "dur": 19.8}, {"key": "b", "label": "The ghost fleet", "trait": "seeker", "reply": "Vepar's ships. So you want to stand on the cliff and watch a hundred sails come out of the weather, knowing none of them is there. That is a taste for the uncanny. It is the right taste for this book, which is one long horizon full of things that only appear.", "audio": "audio/ep08-t1-b.m4a", "dur": 16.2}, {"key": "c", "label": "Only the sound", "trait": "skeptic", "reply": "Crocell, and from a safe distance. Sensible. A noise of water with no water is the one marvel tonight that needs nothing to exist. It is also, I notice, exactly what a frightened person hears in an empty room at night. The book may know more about ears than about angels.", "audio": "audio/ep08-t1-c.m4a", "dur": 19.8}]}, {"i": 15, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Then dry yourself off. The second room is warmer. The thirty-seventh spirit is Phenex, a great marquis with twenty legions. He appears like the phoenix, the bird that burns and rises from its own ashes. And he has the voice of a child.", "start": 281.95, "end": 298.25, "spirit": 37, "mood": "fire", "scene": "fire"}, {"i": 16, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He singeth many sweet notes before the Exorcist, which he must not regard, but by-and-by he must bid him put on Human Shape.", "start": 299.0, "end": 307.6, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 37"}, {"i": 17, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Which he must not regard. The song is so sweet that the book has to warn you off it. Once he takes human shape he speaks marvellously of all the sciences. And the book adds, flatly, that he is a poet, good and excellent. So Hell has a poet, and he reviews well.", "start": 308.65, "end": 327.6}, {"i": 18, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Beside the bird, something with teeth. Haures, the sixty-fourth spirit, a great duke with thirty-six legions. First a leopard, mighty, terrible and strong. Then a man with flaming, fiery eyes and a most terrible face. He tells the truth about past, present and future, but only inside the triangle, that marked space from the third night. Outside it he lies. He burns up the enemies of whoever calls him. And if asked, he will talk about the creation of the world, and how he and the other spirits fell.", "start": 328.35, "end": 362.3, "spirit": 64}, {"i": 19, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "He is not the only one who talks about the fall. Marchosias, the thirty-fifth spirit, a great and mighty marquis with thirty legions. A wolf with a griffin's wings and a serpent's tail, vomiting fire. In the older printed lists the wolf is a she-wolf. He is a strong fighter, and he was once of the order of Dominations. And then the entry does something no personnel file should do.", "start": 363.05, "end": 387.55, "spirit": 35}, {"i": 20, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He told his Chief, who was Solomon, that after 1,200 years he had hopes to return unto the Seventh Throne.", "start": 388.3, "end": 397.6, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 35"}, {"i": 21, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "He had hopes. Stay with me, because this is the heart of the night. Three of tonight's spirits say it. Focalor the drowner, after a thousand years. Marchosias the wolf, after twelve hundred. Phenex the singing bird, after twelve hundred more. Nobody is certain what the Seventh Throne is, and the book never explains. The likeliest reading is a seat in the highest heaven, the place they held before they fell. Home, in other words.", "start": 399.55, "end": 427.4, "mood": "dawn", "scene": "dawn", "term": "seventh-throne"}, {"i": 22, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Think what that does to the catalogue. In the middle of the ranks and the legions, three demons are counting the years. They are homesick. And the older writers would not let it stand. When Reginald Scot printed this list in English in fifteen eighty-four, the wolf's entry ended like this. I have modernised his spelling.", "start": 428.15, "end": 447.25}, {"i": 23, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He was of the order of dominations, under him are thirty legions: he hopeth after twelve hundred years to return to the seventh throne, but he is deceived in that hope.", "start": 448.0, "end": 459.35, "src": "Scot, Discoverie of Witchcraft (1584), spelling modernised"}, {"i": 24, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Deceived in that hope. Somebody felt the need to add it. The Lesser Key, copied a lifetime later, leaves the clause out. The hope simply stands, and no one corrects it. I do not know whether that was mercy or a tired copyist. I know which I prefer.", "start": 460.4, "end": 478.0}, {"i": 25, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep08-t2", "v": "keeper", "t": "So I will ask you what the copyists could not agree on. A wolf, a drowner and a singing bird, all waiting out the centuries. Do the homesick demons deserve to go home?", "start": 478.75, "end": 489.25, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "Let them go home", "trait": "poet", "reply": "Soft heart. You are in old company. Origen, a Christian teacher of the third century, wondered whether in the end every fallen thing might be restored, and the church later condemned the idea. So you have just been gently heretical on behalf of a wolf. I think he would be touched.", "audio": "audio/ep08-t2-a.m4a", "dur": 22.1}, {"key": "b", "label": "No. Scot was right", "trait": "scholar", "reply": "Strict, and orthodox. For most medieval theologians a fallen angel's choice was final, made once and with perfect clarity. No appeal, no parole. Which makes the hope stranger, not weaker. Somebody wrote it down knowing the official answer, and wrote it down anyway.", "audio": "audio/ep08-t2-b.m4a", "dur": 18.7}, {"key": "c", "label": "It's a sales pitch", "trait": "skeptic", "reply": "Sharp. A demon with a sad story is easier to trust, and a book that wants to be used has every reason to make its spirits sympathetic. I cannot rule that out. But notice that it worked on you well enough to need resisting. That is craft, whoever it belonged to.", "audio": "audio/ep08-t2-c.m4a", "dur": 18.4}]}, {"i": 26, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Whatever you decided, leave them to their counting. The third room is the one I promised you, the cabinet of wonders. Shelves, glass, labels. And in the first case, a star.", "start": 491.15, "end": 502.95, "mood": "library", "scene": "library"}, {"i": 27, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Decarabia, the sixty-ninth spirit, a great marquis with thirty legions. He appears as a star in a pentacle, a magical figure drawn as a charm. A point of light inside a diagram. Only when commanded does he put on the image of a man. And the star, of all things, is a naturalist.", "start": 503.7, "end": 521.75, "spirit": 69}, {"i": 28, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "His Office is to discover the Virtues of Birds and Precious Stones, and to make the Similitude of all kinds of Birds to fly before the Exorcist, singing and drinking as natural Birds do.", "start": 522.5, "end": 534.0, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 69"}, {"i": 29, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Where there are birds there is a peacock. Andrealphus, the sixty-fifth, a mighty marquis with thirty legions, arrives as a peacock with great noises. If you have ever heard a peacock, you know that is simple accuracy. He teaches geometry perfectly, and everything to do with measuring and astronomy. And he can transform a man into the likeness of a bird. Whether the man is asked first, the book does not say.", "start": 535.05, "end": 561.15, "spirit": 65}, {"i": 30, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Next case. Oriax, the fifty-ninth, a great marquis with thirty legions. A lion with a serpent's tail, riding a mighty and strong horse, holding two great hissing serpents in his right hand. The old editor could not tell whether the tail belongs to the lion or the horse. Oriax teaches the virtues of the stars and the mansions of the planets, the houses astrologers gave each planet in the sky. He transforms men, and he hands out dignities.", "start": 561.9, "end": 590.7, "spirit": 59}, {"i": 31, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And then the one that stops people. Volac, the sixty-second, a president mighty and great, with thirty-eight legions. He appears like a child with angel's wings, riding on a two-headed dragon. He gives true answers about hidden treasure, and he tells where serpents may be seen. A recent horror film borrowed his other name, Valak, and gave it to a ghostly nun. The book has no nun. The book has a little boy on a dragon, which I find worse.", "start": 591.45, "end": 623.15, "spirit": 62}, {"i": 32, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Last in the cabinet, music. Amdusias, the sixty-seventh, a duke great and strong, with twenty-nine legions. He appears first as a unicorn. Then he stands in human shape, and things begin to be heard. Here is Scot again, in fifteen eighty-four.", "start": 623.9, "end": 641.7, "spirit": 67}, {"i": 33, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He easily bringeth to pass, that trumpets and all musical instruments may be heard and not seen, and also that trees shall bend and incline, according to the conjuror's will.", "start": 642.45, "end": 654.25, "src": "Scot, Discoverie of Witchcraft (1584), spelling modernised"}, {"i": 34, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Heard and not seen. An orchestra with nobody in it, and a forest bowing. By the time the Lesser Key is copied, that line has changed. The instruments are heard, it says, but not soon or immediately. It looks very like a copyist misreading not seen. I cannot prove it. But I enjoy a demon whose miracle became a delay. His seal is in your grimoire now, with eleven others.", "start": 655.3, "end": 680.75}, {"i": 35, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Now, a confession about your eyes. Some of the pictures in your head are not yours. The Lesser Key has no illustrations of the spirits. Only the seals. The famous faces come from Paris, in eighteen sixty-three. You met that engraver's work on the sixth night, in Buer's wheel and the owl of Stolas. His name was Louis Le Breton, and he drew a whole set for a new edition of a demon dictionary by Collin de Plancy. The Dictionnaire Infernal.", "start": 681.5, "end": 709.7, "term": "dictionnaire-infernal"}, {"i": 36, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "If you have ever seen Volac on his dragon, or Amdusias among his trumpets, you have almost certainly seen Le Breton. Two hundred years after the manuscripts. So even the faces of the demons are a later invention. The book gave words. A Frenchman with a sharp pencil gave everyone the same dream.", "start": 710.45, "end": 732.95}, {"i": 37, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "So where did the words get their shapes? Go back to the griffin. Eagle and lion, joined at the waist. That is heraldry, the medieval art of the coat of arms, where a family is a lion, a leopard, a wolf, a unicorn. Now look at tonight's list again. Lion. Leopard. Wolf. Unicorn. Marquises and dukes, each with his own seal. These are coats of arms that walk.", "start": 734.6, "end": 760.0, "mood": "void", "scene": "void", "term": "heraldry"}, {"i": 38, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "That is my reading, not something the book states. But it fits how people drew what nobody had seen. You cannot picture the truly unknown. So you build it from parts you have. A wing from here, a tail from there, a child's voice in a burning bird. The seams are the point. The seams tell you it comes from somewhere else.", "start": 760.75, "end": 782.5}, {"i": 39, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Tomorrow we follow that somewhere else all the way down. Because some of these names were prayed to long before they were feared. Storm gods. Queens of heaven. The ninth night is called Older Than Hell, and it is about how a god becomes a demon.", "start": 783.25, "end": 797.8}, {"i": 40, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Until then. Close the cabinet. If you hear trumpets on the stairs, be patient. They are not soon, or immediately. And if something small sings to you very sweetly on the way up, you know the rule. Do not regard it.", "start": 798.55, "end": 815.2}], "glossary": [{"id": "bestiary", "term": "Bestiary", "def": "A medieval book of beasts, real and imaginary, each described with a picture and a moral. The Goetia's spirits borrow much of their look from the same tradition."}, {"id": "orders", "term": "Orders of Angels", "def": "The nine ranks into which medieval theology sorted the angels, including Thrones, Dominations and Powers. The Goetia often says which order a spirit belonged to before it fell."}, {"id": "seventh-throne", "term": "The Seventh Throne", "def": "The place three spirits of the Goetia say they hope to return to after a thousand or twelve hundred years. The book never explains it; it is usually read as their lost seat in heaven."}, {"id": "dictionnaire-infernal", "term": "Dictionnaire Infernal", "def": "A French dictionary of demons by Collin de Plancy. Its illustrated edition of eighteen sixty-three, with engravings after Louis Le Breton, gave many Goetic spirits the faces people know today, including the wheel-legged Buer and the owl Stolas. Those pictures are not in the Lesser Key."}, {"id": "heraldry", "term": "Heraldry", "def": "The medieval system of coats of arms, in which families and offices were shown as lions, leopards, griffins and other beasts, often stitched together from several animals."}]}, {"id": "ep09", "num": 9, "title": "Older Than Hell", "subtitle": "How a god becomes a demon", "blurb": "A storm god, a goddess, a Persian fury and a Roman door: the older, holier lives of the names in the catalogue.", "mood": "storm", "scene": "storm", "audio": "audio/ep09.m4a", "duration": 727.0, "words": 1837, "segments": [{"i": 0, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Last night I let you admire the animals. Tonight I ask where they came from, and for that we leave the cellar. Stand on a beach with me instead. The coast of Canaan, more than three thousand years ago. The sky is turning the colour of a bruise.", "start": 2.5, "end": 17.5}, {"i": 1, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "The people on this shore are watching the same cloud, and they are not afraid. They are relieved. Because someone is riding that cloud. He carries thunder like a club and lightning like a spear, and when he arrives, the wheat lives. They call him the Rider of the Clouds. His title is Baal. It simply means lord.", "start": 18.25, "end": 39.15, "term": "baal"}, {"i": 2, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "We know this from his own worshippers. Behind you is their city, Ugarit, on what is now the Syrian coast. It burned, and its clay tablets lay in the ruins until a farmer's plough struck stone in nineteen twenty-eight. On them, Baal fights the sea, dies, and returns with the rain. Now remember the first night. A hoarse king who comes as a cat and a toad. Tonight, how gods become demons.", "start": 39.9, "end": 65.8, "term": "ugarit"}, {"i": 3, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Here is the mechanism. When one religion defeats another, the old gods are not deleted. Deleting a god is hard. People know his name, his mountain, his feast days. So you do something cleverer. You keep him, and you demote him. You say, yes, he is real. He is just not what you were told.", "start": 66.55, "end": 84.0}, {"i": 4, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "You can watch it happen in a single word. In Hebrew, the ninety-sixth Psalm says the gods of the nations are idols. Then, in the last centuries before the common era, Jewish scholars in Egypt put their scriptures into Greek. That translation is called the Septuagint. And in the Greek, the word is different.", "start": 84.75, "end": 108.4, "term": "septuagint"}, {"i": 5, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "For the Lord is great, and greatly to be praised: he is terrible above all gods. For all the gods of the heathen are devils: but the Lord made the heavens.", "start": 109.15, "end": 118.55, "src": "Septuagint, Psalm 95 (96 in English Bibles), Brenton translation (1851)"}, {"i": 6, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Devils. In the Greek, daimonia. Not nothing. Not carved wood. Somebody. The early Christians inherited that verse and leaned on it hard. Your neighbour's god is real, and he works for the other side. A conquered god makes an excellent devil. He arrives with a name and a reputation already attached. So let us open some case files.", "start": 119.6, "end": 140.7}, {"i": 7, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Case one, the lord himself. In the Hebrew Bible, Baal is the rival. On Mount Carmel, Elijah challenges his priests to call down fire. Baal loses. Losing is the start of his second career. By the time the name reaches an English catalogue it is spelled Bael, and the storm is gone. The road is not fully mapped, but almost nobody doubts it. I think the hoarse voice is the saddest part. He used to be thunder.", "start": 141.45, "end": 168.2}, {"i": 8, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "The name has a more famous child. In the Book of Kings there is a god called Baal Zebub, lord of the flies. Many scholars suspect a Hebrew sneer at a real title, Baal the Prince. Likely, not proven. By the Gospels, Beelzebub is the prince of devils. And, curiously, he is not among the seventy-two. Baal's most famous descendant never got an entry.", "start": 168.95, "end": 192.9}, {"i": 9, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Case two is crueller. Sail down this coast to Tyre and Sidon, the cities of the Phoenicians. Their great goddess is Astarte. Lady of love and war, close kin to Ishtar of Babylon. The Bible calls her Ashtoreth, and reports with disgust that Solomon himself, in old age, went after her. In the plural, her images are the Ashtaroth.", "start": 194.55, "end": 220.4, "mood": "sea", "scene": "sea"}, {"i": 10, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Now look in your grimoire, at a seal you collected on the third night. Astaroth. A duke. He. A hurtful angel on a dragon, with breath you were warned about. Somewhere between Sidon and the demon lists of Europe, it seems, a Hebrew plural was taken for a proper name, and a goddess became a man. She did not only lose her temples. She lost even her sex in the translation.", "start": 221.15, "end": 247.8}, {"i": 11, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep09-t1", "v": "keeper", "t": "So, my first question tonight. A god is beaten, renamed, and filed under devils. Is he still a god?", "start": 248.55, "end": 256.05, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "Yes. Gods don't resign", "trait": "seeker", "reply": "Then you agree with the people who demoted them, which is the strange part. Nobody in this story says the old gods are gone. The winners insist they are still there, still powerful, still listening. They only changed the label on the door. You and the inquisitors would get along better than either of you would like.", "audio": "audio/ep09-t1-a.m4a", "dur": 20.1}, {"key": "b", "label": "He never was one", "trait": "skeptic", "reply": "Tidy. And notice what follows from it. If Baal was only ever a story about rain, then Bael is only a story about a story, and what we are tracing tonight is how stories behave under pressure. I think that is a perfectly good thing to spend a night on. Stories under pressure is most of history.", "audio": "audio/ep09-t1-b.m4a", "dur": 20.4}, {"key": "c", "label": "Depends who's counting", "trait": "scholar", "reply": "The careful answer, and the one the sources support. In Ugarit he is a god. In the Book of Kings he is a rival. In the Greek Psalms he is a devil. Same name, three ledgers. A historian does not get to pick one. A historian writes down who was holding the pen each time.", "audio": "audio/ep09-t1-c.m4a", "dur": 18.0}]}, {"i": 12, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Keep your answer. Case three travelled furthest. You met Asmoday on the fifth night, the king with three heads. His past begins in ancient Persia, in the religion of Zoroaster, where the demon of wrath is called Aeshma. Most scholars think his name, with the Persian word for demon on the end, travelled west and became Asmodeus. Most, not all. That exact compound is not found in the Persian scriptures, and some specialists reject the link. Likely, then. Not certain.", "start": 257.95, "end": 289.65, "mood": "fire", "scene": "fire", "term": "aeshma"}, {"i": 13, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "What is certain is where he turns up next. The Book of Tobit, a Jewish tale some two centuries older than the Gospels. A young woman named Sarah has been married seven times. Each bridegroom has died on the wedding night, killed by a demon. Asmodeus. The eighth, Tobias, travels with an angel in disguise, whose advice is peculiar. Burn the heart and liver of a fish.", "start": 290.4, "end": 316.6}, {"i": 14, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "The which smell when the evil spirit had smelled, he fled into the utmost parts of Egypt, and the angel bound him.", "start": 317.35, "end": 324.8, "src": "Book of Tobit, chapter 8, King James Version"}, {"i": 15, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Routed by fish smoke. Then the rabbis of the Talmud take him up, and he becomes Ashmedai, king of the demons. Solomon has him brought in chains, to learn how to cut the Temple stones without iron. He helps. Then he tricks the king out of his ring, flings him four hundred leagues, and takes his throne. Persian fury, Jewish folktale, and at last the three-headed king in your grimoire. The best-travelled demon in the book.", "start": 325.85, "end": 355.25}, {"i": 16, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He appeareth like a Wolf with a Serpents tail, vomiting out of his mouth flames of fire.", "start": 356.0, "end": 362.35, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 7"}, {"i": 17, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "That is case four, and it comes with a warning label. Amon, the seventh name in the catalogue. A marquis, with forty legions. A wolf with a serpent's tail, vomiting fire. Ask, and he becomes a man with a raven's head, sometimes with a dog's teeth set in it. He tells all things past and to come. He stirs up feuds, and he reconciles friends who have quarrelled. His seal is in your grimoire now.", "start": 363.4, "end": 389.05, "spirit": 7}, {"i": 18, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Now the name. In Egyptian Thebes, the king of the gods was Amun. It means the hidden one. Greeks called him Ammon, and crossed a desert to question his oracle. Is the wolf the hidden god of Egypt, demoted? Perhaps. The link is suggestive, and it is debated. Others point to Baal Hammon of Carthage. It may be only two names that sound alike. Nobody has the paper trail.", "start": 389.8, "end": 417.6}, {"i": 19, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He appeareth in the Form of a Soldier with Red Clothing, riding upon a Red Horse, and having a Crown of Gold upon his head.", "start": 418.35, "end": 427.4, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 28"}, {"i": 20, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Case five, with a better paper trail. Berith, number twenty-eight. A duke, with twenty-six legions. Red soldier, red horse, gold crown, and a voice the book calls clear and subtle. He answers truly about past, present and future. He turns any metal into gold. He gives dignities and confirms them. And the old catalogues add, quite calmly, that he is a liar. I leave you to decide how much of his gold to accept.", "start": 428.45, "end": 462.7, "spirit": 28}, {"i": 21, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "His name is in the Book of Judges. After Gideon dies, Israel turns to a god called Baal Berith. His temple stands in the town of Shechem. Berith is Hebrew for covenant. Lord of the Covenant. The god you swore your treaties by. The demon's name is almost certainly his. So the keeper of oaths became a spirit who cannot be trusted. That is not just demotion. That is satire.", "start": 463.45, "end": 487.2}, {"i": 22, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep09-t2", "v": "keeper", "t": "Second question. Suppose you could restore one. Hand one fallen god the temple back. Which would it be?", "start": 487.95, "end": 494.6, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "Baal, for the storm", "trait": "poet", "reply": "I hoped someone would. Imagine him getting his voice back. One clap of thunder over the sea, after three thousand years of being a toad with a sore throat. I cannot promise he would be kind about it. Storm gods are not known for letting things go. But it would be something to hear.", "audio": "audio/ep09-t2-a.m4a", "dur": 19.8}, {"key": "b", "label": "Astarte, as herself", "trait": "seeker", "reply": "A just choice. She has the longest list of grievances. Her name mangled, her sex changed, her temples turned into a warning. I suspect she would want the name corrected before anything else. Gods are particular about names. So, as you have noticed by now, are demons.", "audio": "audio/ep09-t2-b.m4a", "dur": 18.5}, {"key": "c", "label": "None. Leave them be", "trait": "skeptic", "reply": "Sensible. A restored god wants things. Festivals, sacrifices, a say in the harvest. A fossil asks for nothing and tells you a great deal. There is a reason museums keep the glass between you and the exhibit, and it is not only to protect the exhibit.", "audio": "audio/ep09-t2-c.m4a", "dur": 18.8}]}, {"i": 23, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Whatever you chose, the last file is about a door. Come in out of the weather, and down. Number forty-six. Bifrons. An earl, with six legions in the printed book, so few that its own editor suspected a slip. He comes first as a monster, and then, when he is told to, as a man. He teaches astrology and geometry, and the virtues of precious stones and woods. And then his entry turns, quietly, to the graveyard.", "start": 496.5, "end": 525.45, "spirit": 46, "mood": "crypt", "scene": "crypt"}, {"i": 24, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He changeth Dead Bodies, and putteth them in another place; also he lighteth seeming Candles upon the Graves of the Dead.", "start": 526.2, "end": 534.05, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 46"}, {"i": 25, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Seeming candles. Now his name. Bifrons is plain Latin. It means two-faced. And it was a well-known title of Janus, the Roman god of doorways and beginnings, who looks both ways at once. The demon is never described with two faces, and nobody can prove the borrowing. But a spirit named for the god of thresholds, who carries the dead across one? I find that hard to call an accident.", "start": 535.1, "end": 563.55, "term": "janus"}, {"i": 26, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "He does not work the graveyard alone. Number twenty-six is Bune, a duke with thirty legions. He is a dragon with three heads, one like a dog, one like a gryphon, one like a man, and he speaks in a high and comely voice. He gives riches, wisdom and eloquence. And he changes the place of the dead, the book says, and makes the spirits under him gather upon your tombs.", "start": 564.3, "end": 591.3, "spirit": 26}, {"i": 27, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And number fifty-four, Murmur. A duke, and the book calls him an earl as well, with thirty legions. A warrior riding a gryphon, in a ducal crown, with his ministers going before him sounding great trumpets. He teaches philosophy perfectly. And he constrains the souls of the dead to come and answer questions. His name is only the Latin for a low sound. A whisper, with a brass band in front of it.", "start": 592.05, "end": 619.15, "spirit": 54}, {"i": 28, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Three keepers of the dead. One moves the bodies, one gathers the spirits on the tombs, one makes the souls speak. No lost god stands behind Bune or Murmur that anyone can name. But the office is ancient. That work is older than any church. The book simply kept it on the payroll.", "start": 619.9, "end": 640.0}, {"i": 29, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "So look at what you are holding. The Lesser Key was copied out in England in the sixteen hundreds, by people who believed they were listing the staff of Hell. Without meaning to, they were also building a museum. Under the seventeenth-century ink there is a Canaanite storm. A Phoenician goddess. Persian wrath. Perhaps an Egyptian mystery. A Roman door.", "start": 641.65, "end": 663.6, "mood": "void", "scene": "void"}, {"i": 30, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "These names were once said kindly. By a farmer who wanted rain. By a bride. By somebody in a doorway at the turn of the year. The prayers stopped, and the names kept travelling, stranger at every border, until they came to rest in a catalogue. Every demon is a fossil of somebody's prayer. You do not have to believe in anything to find that a little haunting.", "start": 664.35, "end": 686.9}, {"i": 31, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Tomorrow, The Men Who Opened the Book. A Dutch doctor who printed a list of demons while defending women accused of witchcraft. An Englishman who reprinted it to show it was all nonsense. And two magicians who came to loathe each other.", "start": 687.65, "end": 703.45}, {"i": 32, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Until then. Five more seals are in your grimoire. A wolf, a red soldier, a monster, a dragon and a man with trumpets. And if it rains on your way home tonight, be civil about it. Somebody was very proud of that, once.", "start": 705.1, "end": 723.2, "mood": "storm", "scene": "storm"}], "glossary": [{"id": "baal", "term": "Baal", "def": "A Semitic word meaning lord or master, and the usual title of the Canaanite storm god Hadad. In the Hebrew Bible he is the great rival of Israel's God; in the Lesser Key the name survives as the first spirit, Bael."}, {"id": "ugarit", "term": "Ugarit", "def": "A Bronze Age port city on the Syrian coast, destroyed about twelve centuries before the common era. Clay tablets dug up there in the twentieth century preserve the myths of Baal in the words of his own worshippers."}, {"id": "septuagint", "term": "Septuagint", "def": "The ancient Greek translation of the Hebrew scriptures, begun in Egypt in the third century before the common era. Its Psalms call the gods of other nations daimonia, demons."}, {"id": "aeshma", "term": "Aeshma", "def": "The demon of wrath in the Zoroastrian religion of ancient Persia. Widely, though not universally, thought to lie behind the name Asmodeus, the Asmoday of the Lesser Key."}, {"id": "janus", "term": "Janus Bifrons", "def": "Janus was the Roman god of doorways, beginnings and endings, shown with two faces looking opposite ways. Bifrons, two-faced, was one of his titles, and is also the name of the forty-sixth spirit."}]}, {"id": "ep10", "num": 10, "title": "The Men Who Opened the Book", "subtitle": "A doctor, a squire, some clerks and a showman", "blurb": "The list of demons was handed down by a sceptic, a mocker, a chain of nameless copyists and Aleister Crowley, and hardly one of them believed in it the way you would expect.", "mood": "library", "scene": "library", "audio": "audio/ep10.m4a", "duration": 727.6, "words": 1803, "segments": [{"i": 0, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Fifteen sixty-three. A town on the lower Rhine. A physician sits down at his desk to write the most dangerous kind of book there is. Not a book of spells. Those are common. This one says the witches are innocent.", "start": 2.5, "end": 16.6}, {"i": 1, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Last night we watched old gods get demoted into demons. Tonight, the people. Because somebody had to write the seventy-two names down, and copy them, and print them. And here is the strange part. Almost none of them believed in the book the way you would expect.", "start": 17.35, "end": 33.85}, {"i": 2, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "His name is Johann Weyer. As a boy he studied under the most notorious magician in Europe, Cornelius Agrippa, and came away with one firm lesson. People swore that Agrippa's black dog was a demon. Weyer had walked it on a lead. It was a dog. He grows up to be court physician to the Duke of Cleves, and he never forgets that dog.", "start": 34.6, "end": 56.5}, {"i": 3, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "His book is called On the Illusions of Demons, and its argument is outrageous for its day. The women being burned across Europe are not sorceresses. They are old, or ill, or deluded, or they were tortured until they said whatever would make it stop. I should be honest. Weyer believes in the Devil. He simply thinks the Devil's best trick is fooling the judges. Treat these women, he says. Do not kill them.", "start": 57.25, "end": 84.85}, {"i": 4, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Then, in fifteen seventy-seven, he adds an appendix. He has got hold of an older manuscript, a catalogue he calls the book of the offices of spirits. An office here means a job. Sixty-nine demons, with their ranks, their shapes and their legions. Where it came from, nobody knows. The manuscript itself is lost.", "start": 85.6, "end": 106.4, "term": "offices-of-spirits"}, {"i": 5, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "He prints it as an exhibit. Look, he is saying, at what the conjurors actually believe. And he gives it a title that is a sneer. Pseudomonarchia Daemonum. The false monarchy of demons. He is careful, too. He tells his reader that he has cut passages out on purpose, the working parts, so that no curious fool can try it. A list of names with the engine removed. Hold on to that. It is the first joke of the night, and it takes a century to land.", "start": 107.15, "end": 135.4, "term": "pseudomonarchia"}, {"i": 6, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Here is one of the sixty-nine, and I choose him because a physician might have liked him. Bathin. A duke, mighty and strong, with thirty legions. He appears as a strong man with the tail of a serpent, sitting on a pale horse.", "start": 136.15, "end": 152.05, "spirit": 18}, {"i": 7, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He knoweth the Virtues of Herbs and Precious Stones, and can transport men suddenly from one country to another.", "start": 152.8, "end": 160.55, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 18"}, {"i": 8, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Herbs and stones. That is an apothecary on a horse. His seal is in your grimoire now. And notice the second gift, sudden transport from one country to another. Because that is exactly what is about to happen to the list.", "start": 161.6, "end": 175.95}, {"i": 9, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep10-t1", "v": "keeper", "t": "Before we cross the water. You are Weyer. You are holding a manuscript of sixty-nine demons, and you think it is poison. What do you do with it?", "start": 176.7, "end": 186.55, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "Print it, teeth pulled", "trait": "scholar", "reply": "Then you are Weyer to the letter. Show the evidence, remove the danger, trust the reader. It is the decent, scholarly thing to do. It is also how the list reached every person who came after him. Good intentions are excellent carriers. Watch what happens to yours.", "audio": "audio/ep10-t1-a.m4a", "dur": 17.6}, {"key": "b", "label": "Burn it", "trait": "skeptic", "reply": "Clean. Final. And I think you would have hesitated with the candle in your hand, the way everyone does. Somebody burned Weyer's source, or lost it, and here we are regardless, because he had already copied it. Burning only works if you are first. Nobody is ever first.", "audio": "audio/ep10-t1-b.m4a", "dur": 20.4}, {"key": "c", "label": "Keep it in a drawer", "trait": "seeker", "reply": "The honest answer. Not published, not destroyed, just kept, for nights when the house is quiet. That is how most of these manuscripts actually survived. Not in libraries. In drawers, belonging to people who would have told you they did not believe a word.", "audio": "audio/ep10-t1-c.m4a", "dur": 17.7}]}, {"i": 10, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Kent, in the south of England. Fifteen eighty-four. A country gentleman named Reginald Scot, whose only other book is about growing hops, is angry. He has watched witch trials in his own county and he thinks them a disgrace. So he writes The Discoverie of Witchcraft. Discoverie in the old sense. Uncovering. Exposure.", "start": 188.45, "end": 210.85, "mood": "dawn", "scene": "dawn", "term": "discoverie"}, {"i": 11, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Scot has read Weyer, and he lifts the whole list into English, so that plain readers can laugh at it. Here is one entry as he printed it. Listen to the bird.", "start": 211.6, "end": 222.5}, {"i": 12, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "Halphas is a great earl, and cometh abroad like a stork, with a hoarse voice, he notably buildeth up towns full of munition and weapons, he sendeth men of war to places appointed, and hath under him six and twenty legions.", "start": 223.25, "end": 237.8, "src": "Scot, The Discoverie of Witchcraft (1584), book 15, spelling modernised"}, {"i": 13, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "A stork. Remember the stork. Now, tradition says that when King James came to the English throne he had every copy of Scot's book burned. It is a fine story, and historians doubt it. No record of any burning has been found. What is documented is that James loathed the book, and said so in print, in his own treatise on demons, where he calls Scot's opinions damnable.", "start": 238.85, "end": 263.4}, {"i": 14, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And here is the second joke. To mock the conjurors, Scot printed their material in plain English, in a book anyone could buy. So for the next hundred years, if you were an English village magician who wanted to raise a spirit, the handiest manual on the market was the book written to stop you. The debunking became the handbook.", "start": 264.15, "end": 285.85}, {"i": 15, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Now the sixteen hundreds, and people with no names at all. Scribes. Clerks. Somebody takes the list, most likely from Scot's English, and starts improving it. They shuffle the order. They draw a seal for every spirit. They lose one, called Pruflas, who had already slipped out of Scot's translation by accident. And they add four that Weyer never had. Vassago, Seere, Dantalion, Andromalius. Sixty-nine becomes seventy-two.", "start": 287.5, "end": 319.05, "mood": "crypt", "scene": "crypt"}, {"i": 16, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "They are, if you will forgive me, fan editors. They have a text they love and they cannot leave it alone. One elaborate copy carries the name of a Doctor Thomas Rudd, and gives every demon an angel to hold its leash. Whether Rudd himself ever touched it is doubted. The copy we have was written out long after his death. But notice what all of them have done. They have put the engine back.", "start": 319.8, "end": 346.9}, {"i": 17, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And things drift, the way they do in copying. Remember the stork? In the finished book he is still Halphas, an earl with twenty-six legions, but now he comes as a stock-dove. A wood pigeon. Nobody knows which pen changed the bird. He still speaks with a hoarse voice. And Scot's towns have become towers. He builds them, furnishes them with ammunition and weapons, and sends men-of-war to places appointed. A pigeon who is an arms contractor.", "start": 347.65, "end": 380.25, "spirit": 38}, {"i": 18, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "His neighbour on the page is the other builder. Malphas. A president, mighty and powerful, with forty legions. He arrives as a crow, and will put on human shape if the conjuror asks, and he too is hoarse. He builds houses and high towers. He tells you what your enemies desire, and think, and have done. He gives good familiars, meaning helpful attendant spirits. And then the book adds a warning.", "start": 381.0, "end": 411.05, "spirit": 39}, {"i": 19, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "If thou makest a Sacrifice unto him he will receive it kindly and willingly, but he will deceive him that doth it.", "start": 411.8, "end": 419.2, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 39"}, {"i": 20, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Receives it kindly, and then deceives. I think of Malphas as the patron of this whole enterprise. Weyer offered the list his contempt. Scot offered it his mockery. The list accepted both, very graciously, and then did exactly as it pleased.", "start": 420.25, "end": 437.55}, {"i": 21, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep10-t2", "v": "keeper", "t": "So pick a side. The doctor, who pulled the teeth out of the book to save lives? Or the nameless copyists, who lovingly put every tooth back?", "start": 438.3, "end": 448.95, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "The doctor", "trait": "skeptic", "reply": "A good side to be on. Weyer's book did real work. Historians still argue over how many lives it touched, but it gave doubt a respectable voice. And yet without the appendix he was faintly ashamed of, you and I would have nothing to talk about tonight. He would hate that. I rather enjoy it.", "audio": "audio/ep10-t2-a.m4a", "dur": 19.2}, {"key": "b", "label": "The copyists", "trait": "poet", "reply": "Of course. They are the ones who loved it. A list of names is only evidence. A list with seals, and circles, and a king with a ring behind it, is a world. They were not being careful. They were being generous. Most of what haunts you in this book is their handwriting.", "audio": "audio/ep10-t2-b.m4a", "dur": 19.0}, {"key": "c", "label": "Whoever kept the drafts", "trait": "scholar", "reply": "The archivist's answer, and the hardest side to join, because almost nobody did keep them. Weyer's source is gone. The first Goetia is gone. What we have are copies of copies, arguing with each other in brown ink. If you enjoy that argument, you are already one of the clerks.", "audio": "audio/ep10-t2-c.m4a", "dur": 20.7}]}, {"i": 22, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Two hundred years go by. The manuscripts wash up where everything washes up, in the British Museum. And in the eighteen eighties a lean, fierce, permanently penniless man sits in its round reading room, copying them out by hand. Samuel Liddell Mathers. He adds MacGregor to his name, for the sake of a Highland ancestry he was very sure of and nobody else was.", "start": 450.85, "end": 476.1, "mood": "library", "scene": "library"}, {"i": 23, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "In eighteen eighty-eight Mathers helps to found the Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn. A secret society for ceremonial magic, in London, with robes, grades and written examinations. Its members include the poet Yeats. And in eighteen ninety-eight it admits a young man with a Cambridge education, a fortune inherited from the family brewery, and no brakes whatever. Aleister Crowley. Mountaineer, chess player, poet of very uneven quality. Mathers thinks him brilliant. Most of the Order cannot stand him.", "start": 476.85, "end": 511.15, "term": "golden-dawn"}, {"i": 24, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Crowley buys a house called Boleskine, above Loch Ness, and begins styling himself its laird. In nineteen hundred the London members revolt against Mathers, and Crowley is sent to seize the Order's rooms. By the usual account he arrives in Highland dress and a black mask. The members fetch a constable. That is the great magical war of London. It ends with a policeman on the stairs.", "start": 512.8, "end": 541.6, "mood": "sea", "scene": "sea"}, {"i": 25, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Then master and pupil quarrel too. And in nineteen oh four, from Boleskine, Crowley publishes Mathers's transcript of the Goetia. The very text the other voice has been reading to you all these nights. Mathers's name is not on the title page. It says the book was translated by a dead hand. Mathers was alive. A note explains that the translator had unhappily succumbed to the assaults of four great princes of the spirits. It may be the rudest preface ever printed.", "start": 542.35, "end": 572.1}, {"i": 26, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "If Crowley has a spirit in this book, it is the twenty-third. Aim. A great strong duke with twenty-six legions. A very handsome man, the book says, but with three heads. The first a serpent's. The second a man's, with two stars on his forehead. The third a calf's, which in Weyer and Scot was a cat's. The copyists again.", "start": 573.75, "end": 596.2, "spirit": 23, "mood": "fire", "scene": "fire"}, {"i": 27, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He rideth on a Viper, carrying a Firebrand in his Hand, wherewith he setteth cities, castles, and great Places, on fire.", "start": 596.95, "end": 607.2, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 23"}, {"i": 28, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And when he is not burning cities, Aim makes you witty in all manner of ways, and gives true answers about private matters. Set fire to the place and be amusing about it. That is very nearly Crowley's whole career.", "start": 608.25, "end": 622.7}, {"i": 29, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Then Crowley does the strangest thing of all. He opens the book with an essay of his own. And in it the wickedest man in England, as the newspapers would later call him, calmly explains that there are no demons.", "start": 624.35, "end": 637.7, "mood": "void", "scene": "void"}, {"i": 30, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "The spirits of the Goetia are portions of the human brain.", "start": 638.45, "end": 642.5, "src": "Crowley, The Initiated Interpretation of Ceremonial Magic, in Goetia (1904)"}, {"i": 31, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Portions of the human brain. The seals, he says, are ways of stirring those portions through the eye. Whether he believed it for long is another matter. Crowley believed a great many things, often before breakfast. But the idea outlived him, and tomorrow it gets the whole night.", "start": 643.55, "end": 662.05, "term": "initiated-interpretation"}, {"i": 32, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "So count them up. A doctor who printed the list to prove it false. A squire who translated it to laugh at it. Clerks who tinkered with it like a hobby. A showman who took it and then explained it away. Hardly a simple believer in the chain. This book did not survive on faith. It survived because nobody, however sensible, could quite bring himself to throw it away. It is too interesting. You are on your tenth night. You know.", "start": 662.8, "end": 692.25}, {"i": 33, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Tomorrow, The Demon in the Mirror. If the spirits are portions of the brain, then whose brain? We follow the seventy-two into psychology, into films and games, and we ask the sceptic's question fairly. Why does this still pull?", "start": 693.0, "end": 709.4}, {"i": 34, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Until then. Close the cover gently. Cleverer people than us have failed to keep it shut. And if a crow lands on your windowsill tonight and offers to build you a house, be civil. Just do not give him anything.", "start": 710.15, "end": 723.8}], "glossary": [{"id": "offices-of-spirits", "term": "Book of the Offices of Spirits", "def": "The older manuscript catalogue of demons that Johann Weyer says he copied. It is lost; an office here means a spirit's job or function."}, {"id": "pseudomonarchia", "term": "Pseudomonarchia Daemonum", "def": "The False Monarchy of Demons. A list of sixty-nine spirits that the physician Johann Weyer added to his book against witch-hunting in 1577. The main ancestor of the Goetia."}, {"id": "discoverie", "term": "The Discoverie of Witchcraft", "def": "Reginald Scot's sceptical book of 1584, which put Weyer's list of demons into English in order to mock it, and was then used as a source by magicians."}, {"id": "golden-dawn", "term": "Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn", "def": "A London society for ceremonial magic founded in 1888 by William Wynn Westcott, Samuel Liddell MacGregor Mathers and William Robert Woodman. Yeats and Crowley were both members."}, {"id": "initiated-interpretation", "term": "The Initiated Interpretation", "def": "Aleister Crowley's short essay at the front of the 1904 Goetia, which argues that the spirits are parts of the human brain and the ritual a way of stimulating them."}]}, {"id": "ep11", "num": 11, "title": "The Demon in the Mirror", "subtitle": "Seventy-two faces, and one of them is yours", "blurb": "Crowley moves the demons out of Hell and into your head, the sceptic takes the floor, and a four-hundred-year-old list becomes a cast of characters.", "mood": "void", "scene": "void", "audio": "audio/ep11.m4a", "duration": 693.4, "words": 1870, "segments": [{"i": 0, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Picture a man walking toward you out of the dark, with a book in his right hand. He has a face. Then you see that he has another. And another. Men's faces, women's faces, all of them his, all of them looking at you. The book calls him a duke. And it says he knows what you are thinking.", "start": 2.5, "end": 19.9}, {"i": 1, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "His Office is to teach all Arts and Sciences unto any; and to declare the Secret Counsel of any one; for he knoweth the Thoughts of all Men and Women, and can change them at his Will.", "start": 20.65, "end": 32.0, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 71"}, {"i": 2, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Dantalion. The seventy-first spirit, a great and mighty duke with thirty-six legions, a man with many faces and a book in his hand. He teaches every art and science. He causes love. He can show you the likeness of any person, wherever in the world they are. And he knows the thoughts of all men and women, and can change them. Now be honest with me. Does that sound like a monster? Or does it sound like something you already carry in your pocket? His seal is in your grimoire now.", "start": 33.05, "end": 62.7, "spirit": 71}, {"i": 3, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Good evening. This is the eleventh night. Last night we met the men who opened the book, and tonight we keep one of them a little longer. Because in nineteen oh four Aleister Crowley did something to this book that it has never recovered from. He moved the demons. Not out of Hell. Into you. He did it in that short, swaggering essay at the front of his edition. You heard one line of it last night. Here it is again, with the swagger left in.", "start": 64.35, "end": 92.75, "mood": "library", "scene": "library"}, {"i": 4, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "Say you so? I can, and will. The spirits of the Goetia are portions of the human brain.", "start": 93.5, "end": 100.4, "src": "Crowley, The Initiated Interpretation of Ceremonial Magic, 1904"}, {"i": 5, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Portions of the human brain. He gives an example, too. When Solomon says a certain spirit teaches logic, Crowley says, what that really means is that the logical parts of my own brain can be woken up and trained by the ceremony that carries that spirit's name. People now call this the psychological model. The seventy-two are not a census of Hell. They are a map of one head. Its appetites, its talents, its fears.", "start": 101.45, "end": 129.4, "term": "psychological-model"}, {"i": 6, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "On this reading, everything you met on the third night changes its meaning. The circle is not a wall against anything. It is concentration, a line drawn around your attention. The triangle is not a cage. It is a place to put a feeling, outside yourself, so that you can finally look at it. And the conjuror shouting names in the dark is a person having a very stern conversation with himself.", "start": 130.15, "end": 156.2}, {"i": 7, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Years later, the Swiss psychiatrist Carl Jung described something he called the shadow. Everything in you that you would rather not own. The greed, the rage, the wanting. Ignore it, he said, and it does not leave. It waits. Jung was not writing about this book. But readers of this book heard an echo, and they have been borrowing his word ever since.", "start": 156.95, "end": 177.35, "term": "shadow"}, {"i": 8, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Now the honest part. This is one reading among several, and Crowley himself wavered on it later. People who believed in the older sense, who thought something truly came, would call it a dodge. A way to keep the thrill and drop the risk. Nobody four hundred years ago fasted and trembled in a circle in order to meet his own bad temper. Keep that objection. It is a good one.", "start": 178.1, "end": 203.35}, {"i": 9, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep11-t1", "v": "keeper", "t": "So let me ask you the question this whole night turns on. The seventy-two. Where do you think they live?", "start": 204.1, "end": 210.3, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "Out there, somewhere", "trait": "seeker", "reply": "Then you are in old company. Nearly everyone who copied this book before nineteen hundred agreed with you, and took precautions accordingly. I will not tell you that you are wrong. I will only say that nothing out there has ever been caught on its way in. Hold the thought loosely, and the lamp tightly.", "audio": "audio/ep11-t1-a.m4a", "dur": 21.6}, {"key": "b", "label": "In here, in my head", "trait": "skeptic", "reply": "Crowley's answer, and the tidy one. Nothing to prove, and nothing to fear but yourself. Though I notice that is not a small thing to fear. If they live in your head, you have been carrying all seventy-two around since you were born, and nobody ever gave you the list. Until now.", "audio": "audio/ep11-t1-b.m4a", "dur": 17.2}, {"key": "c", "label": "On the page", "trait": "poet", "reply": "The librarian's answer, and secretly my favourite. They live in ink. They are as real as Hamlet, who never existed and has outlived everyone who ever watched him. A thing on a page can still change a life. Ask anyone who was handed the right book at the wrong age.", "audio": "audio/ep11-t1-c.m4a", "dur": 19.2}]}, {"i": 10, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Wherever you put them, the book seems to have known that the mind was the real battleground. Because it holds a spirit whose whole trick is belief. Ose. A great president with three legions in the printed text, though its editor suspected thirty was meant. He comes first as a leopard, and after a little while puts on the shape of a man. He makes you clever in the liberal sciences, which is the old name for a proper education, and answers truly about divine and secret things. And then there is his other gift.", "start": 212.2, "end": 244.5, "spirit": 57, "mood": "court", "scene": "court", "term": "liberal-sciences"}, {"i": 11, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "to change a Man into any Shape that the Exorcist pleaseth, so that he that is so changed will not think any other thing than that he is in verity that Creature or Thing he is changed into.", "start": 245.25, "end": 256.75, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 57"}, {"i": 12, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Read that slowly. He does not simply turn you into a wolf. He makes you unable to think you are anything else. The older version, the one Weyer printed and Scot put into English, is blunter. There, he brings a man to such madness that he thinks he is a king, or a pope, or that he wears a crown on his head. And it lasts, the old text says, one hour.", "start": 257.8, "end": 283.05}, {"i": 13, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "One hour of being pope. I think that is the book's own small parable about delusion, and I do not think it is unkind. Belief is not a fault in foolish people. It is what minds do. Tell a mind firmly enough what it is and it will move in, and furnish the place. The crown feels heavy. The crowd sounds real. The only tell is that nobody else can see it.", "start": 283.8, "end": 306.8}, {"i": 14, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Which brings us to the sceptic, who deserves the floor, and deserves it without a sneer. Here is the case at full strength. In all the centuries of this book, no spirit has ever been demonstrated. Not once, under any conditions a stranger could check. Every account we have is one person's word, often written down long afterwards.", "start": 308.45, "end": 331.25, "mood": "crypt", "scene": "crypt"}, {"i": 15, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And the experiences have ordinary explanations. Take someone who expects to see something. Keep them awake. Fill a small room with incense and one moving candle flame, frighten them thoroughly, and ask them to stare into an empty triangle. A human brain in that state will supply a face. That is suggestion, and it needs no devil. Notice that Crowley and the sceptic agree on nearly all of it.", "start": 332.0, "end": 358.85, "term": "suggestion"}, {"i": 16, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "So why does the catalogue still comfort people? Because naming a fear makes it smaller. A dread with no name is the size of the whole night. A dread with a name, a rank and a count of legions is the size of a paragraph. That is what a list is. A spell against chaos. Seventy-two is a great many demons. But you can count to it.", "start": 359.6, "end": 381.6}, {"i": 17, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And look what has become of the list. In twenty eighteen a horror film called Hereditary put the name Paimon in front of millions of people who had never heard the word grimoire. You met him on the fifth night. He was only the latest. For decades, video games, role-playing games, comics, animation and heavy metal have borrowed these names by the armful. Stolas, from the sixth night, now usually drawn as an owl, has become oddly beloved online.", "start": 383.25, "end": 413.2, "mood": "fire", "scene": "fire"}, {"i": 18, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Think how strange that is. A manuscript four hundred years old, copied by hand and kept well hidden, is now a shared cast of characters, like the Greek gods or the knights of the Round Table. Nobody owns them. And the modern eye reads them the way it reads any character. As a type. As a mirror. Let me show you, with two riders.", "start": 413.95, "end": 433.7}, {"i": 19, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "His Face is like that of a Lion, very Red, and having Flaming Eyes. His Speech is hoarse and very big.", "start": 434.45, "end": 444.2, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 52"}, {"i": 20, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Alloces. A duke, great and strong, with thirty-six legions. He comes as a soldier on a great horse, with that red lion's face and that enormous hoarse voice. And what does this furnace of a creature do? He teaches astronomy and all the liberal sciences, and brings good familiars, meaning helpful attendant spirits. A modern reader knows him at once. The terrifying teacher, who shouted, and was right, and whom you never forgot. That is an archetype. A figure so old and so common that everyone has met one.", "start": 445.25, "end": 479.05, "spirit": 52, "term": "archetype"}, {"i": 21, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "appearing like a Valiant Warrior riding upon a goodly Black Horse. He ruleth over all Spirits in the parts of Africa.", "start": 479.8, "end": 488.4, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 66"}, {"i": 22, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Cimejes. A marquis, mighty and strong, a valiant warrior on a fine black horse. He has twenty legions behind him, a modest command for so grand a rider. He teaches grammar, logic and rhetoric perfectly, and he finds things lost or hidden, treasure included. He is the very spirit Crowley chose for his example, the one who teaches logic. A knight who corrects your grammar and brings back what you lost. Half the fantasy novels on your shelf have him in them.", "start": 489.45, "end": 520.95, "spirit": 66}, {"i": 23, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "But look again at one line. He rules all spirits in the parts of Africa. Handle that with care. It tells you nothing about Africa. It tells you about the Europeans who wrote it, for whom a whole continent was a far edge of the map, where a demon marquis might as well be governor. The book is a mirror there too. It shows the mind that made it, blind spots included.", "start": 521.7, "end": 545.7}, {"i": 24, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep11-t2", "v": "keeper", "t": "Seventy-two faces in the glass, and I think one of them always looks a little familiar. Of the ones you met tonight, which face is yours?", "start": 546.45, "end": 556.2, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "The one with many faces", "trait": "poet", "reply": "Dantalion. Then you are a slightly different person in every room, and you watch people closely enough to guess what they are thinking. That is not dishonesty. It is attention. Only remember that he carries a book. Even the reader of minds has to look things up.", "audio": "audio/ep11-t2-a.m4a", "dur": 16.8}, {"key": "b", "label": "The finder of lost things", "trait": "scholar", "reply": "Cimejes. The patient one, with the grammar and the black horse. You are the person people call when something has gone missing. A fact, a file, a word on the tip of the tongue. It is an unglamorous gift, and the world runs on it. Ride carefully.", "audio": "audio/ep11-t2-b.m4a", "dur": 17.0}, {"key": "c", "label": "The leopard who believes", "trait": "seeker", "reply": "Ose. Brave of you. You know how it feels to become a thing entirely, for an hour, and to come back blinking. Actors know it. So do people in love. The trick, the old text hints, is the coming back. Keep one hand on the door.", "audio": "audio/ep11-t2-c.m4a", "dur": 18.6}]}, {"i": 25, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Whichever face you chose, notice what you just did. You looked down a list of devils and found yourself in it, in a few seconds. That is the oldest use of this book, and nobody admits to it. Crowley, in a snappish aside in that same essay, says the world of magic is a mirror, and that whoever sees muck in it is muck. Rude. Not wrong.", "start": 557.2, "end": 583.35}, {"i": 26, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "So, a direct word, if you are new and you feel the pull. Curiosity is fine. It may be the best thing about you. Read widely. Read the believers, the debunkers and the historians, and notice where they disagree. And be wary of anyone selling certainty. Anyone who wants money for the next secret, or wants your obedience, has told you all you need to know about them.", "start": 584.1, "end": 609.85}, {"i": 27, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "This book has always been safest in the hands of people who can hold two thoughts at once. That it is a remarkable human document, and that nothing in it has ever been shown to be true. That it is paperwork, and that it raises the hair on your arms. You have been doing it for eleven nights.", "start": 610.6, "end": 631.75}, {"i": 28, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Here is where I land. Whether the seventy-two live in a lake in Babylon or in the folds of your own head, the book works the same way. You open it. You read a name. And something looks back that you half recognise. It is a mirror with seventy-two faces. Dantalion was never the odd one out. He was the book's self-portrait.", "start": 633.4, "end": 656.1, "mood": "void", "scene": "void"}, {"i": 29, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Tomorrow is the last night. The License to Depart. Every ceremony in this book ends the same way, with a formal and rather courteous goodbye, and so shall we. There are three names left to say. Then we close the circle, and I tell you where to read next.", "start": 656.85, "end": 674.1}, {"i": 30, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Until then. Sixty-nine seals in your grimoire, if you have been paying attention. Go and wash your face, and have a look in the glass while you are there. Count how many look back. If it is more than one, be polite. Say good night to all of them.", "start": 674.85, "end": 689.6}], "glossary": [{"id": "psychological-model", "term": "The Psychological Model", "def": "The modern reading, launched by Aleister Crowley in nineteen oh four, that the spirits are parts of the magician's own mind rather than outside beings. One interpretation among several."}, {"id": "shadow", "term": "The Shadow", "def": "Carl Jung's name for the parts of yourself you would rather not admit to. Later readers of the Goetia borrowed the idea; Jung was not writing about this book."}, {"id": "liberal-sciences", "term": "Liberal Sciences", "def": "The old school curriculum of seven subjects: grammar, logic, rhetoric, arithmetic, geometry, music and astronomy. A surprising number of the seventy-two teach them."}, {"id": "suggestion", "term": "Suggestion", "def": "The ordinary tendency of a mind to see and feel what it has been led to expect, especially when tired, frightened or staring into the dark."}, {"id": "archetype", "term": "Archetype", "def": "A stock figure that turns up again and again in stories everywhere, such as the stern teacher, the trickster or the knight."}]}, {"id": "ep12", "num": 12, "title": "The License to Depart", "subtitle": "Three last names, and how to say goodbye to a demon", "blurb": "The grimoire fills to seventy-two of seventy-two, the book circles back to its brass jar, and the Keeper shows you the most important page in it: the one about leaving.", "mood": "crypt", "scene": "crypt", "audio": "audio/ep12.m4a", "duration": 675.0, "words": 1784, "segments": [{"i": 0, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Last night I left you with a mirror. Tonight I owe you a door. Because every summoning in this book ends the same way. Not with a bang. Not with fire, or a scream, or a bargain signed in anything red. It ends with good manners. The magician thanks the spirit. And then, politely and firmly, he tells it to go home.", "start": 2.5, "end": 23.15}, {"i": 1, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "This is the twelfth night, and the last. Your grimoire has three empty places left in it. We are going to fill them, close the book the way it closes itself, and then I will walk you to the stairs. Bring the lamp. Three names.", "start": 23.9, "end": 39.95}, {"i": 2, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "The first has been waiting a long time. Number forty-three. Sabnock. A Marquis, mighty, great and strong, with fifty legions. He comes as an armed soldier with a lion's head, riding a pale horse. And his office is building. High towers, castles, whole cities, and he furnishes them with armour. He also gives good familiars, a familiar being a small attendant spirit that stays at your side. You have met builders on other nights. He is the last of them.", "start": 40.7, "end": 72.6, "spirit": 43, "term": "familiar"}, {"i": 3, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And because this book never gives with one hand only, the same entry says he afflicts men for many days with wounds and sores. He raises the walls and rots the men behind them. Anyone who has read about a siege will call that an accurate description of a fortress.", "start": 73.35, "end": 92.85}, {"i": 4, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Now leave the walls behind. Number seventy. Seere. A mighty Prince, with twenty-six legions, and he appears as a beautiful man riding a winged horse. His office, the book says, is to go and come. That is nearly all. He carries things where you want them. He brings an abundance of things to pass on a sudden. And he is fast.", "start": 94.5, "end": 117.6, "spirit": 70, "mood": "sea", "scene": "sea"}, {"i": 5, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "He can pass over the whole Earth in the twinkling of an Eye. He giveth a True relation of all sorts of Theft, and of Treasure hid, and of many other things.", "start": 118.35, "end": 128.35, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 70"}, {"i": 6, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "He is of an indifferent good nature, the book adds, and willing to do anything the exorcist desires. The exorcist is simply the book's word for the magician. And indifferent does not mean he does not care. It is the old sense. Fairly. Tolerably good-natured. After sixty-nine entries of fire, flattery and worms, here is a spirit whose worst quality is being obliging. He may be the gentlest thing in these pages. He is practically a courier.", "start": 129.4, "end": 157.6, "term": "exorcist"}, {"i": 7, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And then the last. Number seventy-two. Andromalius. An Earl, great and mighty, with thirty-six legions. He appears as a man holding a great serpent in his hand. No horse. No crown. No trumpets going before him. Just a man and a snake. He is the book's thief-taker, and its finder of hidden treasure. Listen.", "start": 159.25, "end": 181.6, "spirit": 72, "mood": "court", "scene": "court"}, {"i": 8, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "His Office is to bring back both a Thief, and the Goods which be stolen; and to discover all Wickedness, and Underhand Dealing; and to punish all Thieves and other Wicked People and also to discover Treasures that be Hid.", "start": 182.35, "end": 197.25, "src": "Goetia (1904), spirit 72"}, {"i": 9, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "A catalogue of seventy-two devils, and the very last one catches thieves. He returns what was taken. The list that opened with a king who makes you invisible closes with an earl who finds you out. Joke, warning, or accident, I cannot tell you. But the list ends on a demon of justice.", "start": 198.3, "end": 218.45}, {"i": 10, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "One honest footnote, since it is the last night. These final names are latecomers. When the doctor, Johann Weyer, printed his list of spirits in fifteen seventy-seven, Seere was not on it. Neither was Andromalius. They turn up in the English manuscripts of the following century, and nobody knows where the copyists found them. Somebody, quite late, decided the list needed an ending.", "start": 219.2, "end": 244.15, "term": "pseudomonarchia"}, {"i": 11, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Now look at your grimoire. Go on. If you have kept every night, there is no blank left in it. Seventy-two of seventy-two. Every seal drawn. It took Solomon a ring and an angel. It took you twelve evenings and a little nerve.", "start": 244.9, "end": 260.5}, {"i": 12, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep12-t1", "v": "keeper", "t": "Seventy-two faces, and you will not keep them all. Nobody does. So tell me. When the rest have faded, which one will you remember?", "start": 261.25, "end": 270.4, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "The hoarse king", "trait": "seeker", "reply": "Bael. The first one through the door. People do remember the first, the way you remember the first step down into cold water. If you ever hear a cat, a toad and a man clear their throats at once, be civil. Tell him the Keeper sends her regards. He will know who you mean.", "audio": "audio/ep12-t1-a.m4a", "dur": 19.8}, {"key": "b", "label": "The homesick phoenix", "trait": "poet", "reply": "I hoped someone would say that. A bird with a child's voice, counting the centuries until it might be let back in. Of all of them, that is the one I would least like to forget. Keep it somewhere warm. Things that want to go home deserve to be remembered by somebody.", "audio": "audio/ep12-t1-b.m4a", "dur": 16.8}, {"key": "c", "label": "The doctor, frankly", "trait": "skeptic", "reply": "Weyer. Of course. The one name on the shelf that belonged to a living man, who copied out a list of demons to argue for mercy toward real people. You came for the evidence, and I respect it. He would have liked you. He would have asked for your sources first.", "audio": "audio/ep12-t1-c.m4a", "dur": 16.7}]}, {"i": 13, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Hold on to that one. Now watch what the book does when it runs out of names. It does not stop. Straight after Andromalius, without so much as a blank line, it tells a story. And it is a story you already know.", "start": 272.3, "end": 287.15, "mood": "crypt", "scene": "crypt"}, {"i": 14, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "These be the seventy-two mighty Kings and Princes which King Solomon commanded into a Vessel of Brass, together with their Legions.", "start": 287.9, "end": 296.05, "src": "Goetia (1904), closing note"}, {"i": 15, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "You heard that line on the first night. It was the first thing the book ever said to you. What I only mentioned in passing then is that it comes at the end of the list, not the beginning. The jar, the pride, the lake, the broken seal. The catalogue finishes by explaining why there is a catalogue. The snake has its tail in its mouth. We began where it ends.", "start": 297.1, "end": 318.3}, {"i": 16, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And look who came out of that jar for you. A hoarse king who was a cat and a toad and a man all at once. A phoenix with a child's voice, hoping to go home. A duke with every face there is. And walking among them, entirely human, a doctor who printed the whole list while arguing that the women being burned as witches were innocent. History, as ever, is where the bodies are.", "start": 319.05, "end": 344.0}, {"i": 17, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "So. The names are said. In the book's imagination the spirit has come, and has answered every question. What now? This is the part the films leave out. The text is clear that whatever has been called must be sent. And the sending has a name. The License to Depart. It is the last thing the magician says to the spirit. He speaks its name, and then this.", "start": 344.75, "end": 369.75, "term": "license-to-depart"}, {"i": 18, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "Because thou hast diligently answered unto my demands, and hast been very ready and willing to come at my call, I do here license thee to depart unto thy proper place; without causing harm or danger unto man or beast.", "start": 370.5, "end": 386.1, "src": "Goetia (1904), The License to Depart"}, {"i": 19, "kind": "say", "v": "book", "t": "Depart, then, I say, and be thou very ready to come at my call, being duly exorcised and conjured by the sacred rites of magic. I charge thee to withdraw peaceably and quietly, and the peace of God be ever continued between thee and me. Amen.", "start": 387.15, "end": 403.8, "src": "Goetia (1904), The License to Depart"}, {"i": 20, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Listen to the temperature of that. After pages of threats, of chains and fire and terrible names, the last word to a demon is very nearly thank you. You answered. Go home, and hurt no one on the way, man or beast. And then the line I can never quite get past. The peace of God be ever continued between thee and me. Between a magician and a devil. Peace.", "start": 404.85, "end": 429.55}, {"i": 21, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "The book adds one more rule, and I report it as history, not as advice. The magician is not to leave his circle until the spirit has gone. That fear runs through the old stories. The apprentice who can start the thing and cannot stop it. The tales are almost never about the summoning. They are about the goodbye somebody forgot to say.", "start": 430.3, "end": 451.25}, {"i": 22, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Which is why I think this is the most important page in a book about demons. Anyone can open a door. The rare skill is closing one. Saying, that is enough, and meaning it. Read the Lesser Key as psychology, as we did last night, and the License is the moment you put the dark thing down and it stays down. Read it as literature, and it is simply a good ending. Firm, courteous, and short.", "start": 452.0, "end": 479.85}, {"i": 23, "kind": "threshold", "id": "ep12-t2", "v": "keeper", "t": "Which means it is nearly time for yours. But first. You have spent twelve nights down here with me. What do you take with you up the stairs?", "start": 480.6, "end": 490.45, "opts": [{"key": "a", "label": "The history", "trait": "scholar", "reply": "Then you leave heavier than you came, and better armed. Manuscripts, forgeries, a doctor, a sceptic, two quarrelling magicians. You know now that every demon has a paper trail. Follow it. And when you find the mistake I surely made somewhere in these twelve nights, be kind about it. Go well, scholar.", "audio": "audio/ep12-t2-a.m4a", "dur": 21.9}, {"key": "b", "label": "The shiver", "trait": "seeker", "reply": "Good. Keep it. That small cold drop in the stomach is the oldest thing this book sells, and you paid for it honestly, by listening in the dark. It will fade by breakfast. It always does. But you will know where the stairs are if you ever want it back. Go well, seeker.", "audio": "audio/ep12-t2-b.m4a", "dur": 22.1}, {"key": "c", "label": "Only the manners", "trait": "poet", "reply": "Then you have taken the best thing in it. Not a name, not a seal. Just the idea that even the worst guest should be thanked, and shown out gently, and wished peace at the door. Most people leave here with a demon. You are leaving with a courtesy. Go well, poet.", "audio": "audio/ep12-t2-c.m4a", "dur": 16.7}]}, {"i": 24, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Then you still have a hand free, and I would like to put some books in it. If you want to keep reading, here is where a beginner should go next. No page numbers. Just a shelf.", "start": 492.35, "end": 502.8, "mood": "library", "scene": "library"}, {"i": 25, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "For the Lesser Key itself, the scholarly edition by Joseph Peterson. He went back to the old handwritten copies and shows you where they disagree. For the bigger story, a history by Owen Davies, called simply Grimoires. It follows books of magic from the ancient world to the paperback rack, and it is better company than it has any right to be.", "start": 503.55, "end": 525.65}, {"i": 26, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Then the old voices. The Testament of Solomon, where the ring and the interviews begin. It is short, strange, and free to read online. So are Johann Weyer and Reginald Scot, the two doubters who printed the list of spirits and, by printing it, preserved it. And for the pictures, the Dictionnaire Infernal, a French dictionary of demons whose illustrated edition gave these spirits the faces most people now imagine.", "start": 526.4, "end": 557.9}, {"i": 27, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "And one piece of advice, the same one I gave you last night. Read the sceptics and the believers side by side. The magician next to the historian. Each of them is right about something the other cannot see, and the truth about this book lives in the gap between their chairs. Anyone who offers you only one of them is selling something.", "start": 558.65, "end": 579.55}, {"i": 28, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "One more thing before the stairs. There is a page in your grimoire marked You. A confession. Every time I asked you a question on these nights, I kept a note of your answer. Scholar, seeker, sceptic, poet. It is all there. And on that page you can give it any word you like, your own name even, and it will draw you a seal of your own. It summons nothing. It is a keepsake. A pressed flower from a strange garden.", "start": 580.3, "end": 605.75}, {"i": 29, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Now. Look up. There is grey light at the top of the stairs. It is always night down here, and I have rather lost the habit of mornings. But the lamp is paler than it was, and that means it is time. I know the form. So stand there a moment, and let me do this properly.", "start": 607.4, "end": 626.1, "mood": "dawn", "scene": "dawn"}, {"i": 30, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "Listener. Because you have diligently attended to my stories, and have been very ready and willing to come at my call, twelve nights running, I do here license you to depart unto your proper place. Without harm or danger to man or beast. Go peaceably. Go quietly. And let there be peace, always, between you and me.", "start": 626.85, "end": 648.2}, {"i": 31, "kind": "say", "v": "keeper", "t": "The seventy-two are in your grimoire, and they will keep. You know their names now. You know what each is said to do, who wrote it down, and who doubted. That is all a key ever was. Come back whenever you like. I will be here, holding the lamp. Mind the stairs on your way up. And for the first time in twelve nights, I get to say it. Good morning.", "start": 648.95, "end": 671.2}], "glossary": [{"id": "familiar", "term": "Familiar", "def": "An attendant spirit said to serve a magician. Several spirits of the Goetia are described as giving good familiars."}, {"id": "exorcist", "term": "Exorcist", "def": "In the Goetia, simply the magician: the person who calls and commands a spirit. The modern meaning, someone who drives a spirit out, came to dominate later."}, {"id": "pseudomonarchia", "term": "Weyer's List", "def": "The False Monarchy of Demons. A list of sixty-nine spirits that the physician Johann Weyer added to his book against witch-hunting in 1577. The main ancestor of the Goetia."}, {"id": "license-to-depart", "term": "License to Depart", "def": "The formal dismissal that ends every working in the book: the spirit is thanked and told to return peaceably to its place, harming no one."}]}];
